-- Migration: Update template guidelines with full content from .txt files
-- Adds 'instructions' field and detailed multi-line guidelines for each section

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a direct, imperative reference document that an AI coding assistant will read before every task. Use short declarative sentences and bullet points. Be specific about file paths, naming conventions, and patterns. Include concrete examples of do/don''t for each rule. The tone should be a technical style guide, not a tutorial. Every rule should be actionable and unambiguous.", "sections": [{"name": "Project Overview", "guidelines": "Brief description of what the project does and its current maturity.\n\n### Repository Structure\nDescribe the top-level directory layout and where key code lives.\n\n### Project Status\n- **Maturity**: Prototype / MVP / Production\n- **Key technologies**: List the core frameworks and languages\n- **Monorepo?**: If yes, describe the workspace structure"}, {"name": "Tech Stack & Conventions", "guidelines": "List every major technology with its version and any project-specific conventions.\n\n- **Framework**: e.g., Next.js 14 (App Router only — no Pages Router)\n- **Language**: e.g., TypeScript (strict mode)\n- **Package manager**: e.g., pnpm\n- **Database**: e.g., PostgreSQL via Neon\n- **ORM**: e.g., Prisma\n- **Auth**: e.g., Clerk\n- **Hosting**: e.g., Vercel\n- **UI**: e.g., shadcn/ui + Tailwind CSS\n- **State management**: e.g., React Query for server state, Zustand for client\n\nFor each, note project-specific conventions: ''Prefer server components by default,'' ''Use App Router route handlers, not API routes,'' etc."}, {"name": "Code Style Rules", "guidelines": "### Naming Conventions\n- **Files (components)**: PascalCase (e.g., `MessageDisplay.tsx`)\n- **Files (non-components)**: kebab-case (e.g., `use-auth.ts`)\n- **Directories**: kebab-case (e.g., `chat-messages/`)\n- **Variables/functions**: camelCase\n- **Types/interfaces**: PascalCase\n- **Database fields**: camelCase\n\n### Imports & Exports\n- Import order: React, third-party, local (with blank line between groups)\n- Prefer named exports over default exports\n\n### TypeScript\n- Use strict types — avoid `any`, prefer `unknown` when needed\n- Define return types explicitly on exported functions\n\n### Formatting\n- Specify linter/formatter (ESLint, Prettier, Biome) and key config choices"}, {"name": "Component & Architecture Patterns", "guidelines": "### Component Structure\n- Where components live and how they are organized\n- Preferred component pattern (functional only, with hooks)\n- Props typing approach (inline vs. separate interface)\n\n### Data Fetching\n- Server components vs. client components: when to use which\n- Data fetching library and patterns (React Query, SWR, server actions)\n- Loading and error state handling\n\n### State Management\n- Server state: approach and library\n- Client state: approach and library\n- Form state: library and validation approach (e.g., React Hook Form + Zod)\n\n### API Routes\n- Input validation pattern (e.g., Zod schemas)\n- Error response format\n- Authentication middleware approach"}, {"name": "Do''s and Don''ts", "guidelines": "Explicit rules the AI must follow. Be specific and exhaustive — this is the highest-impact section.\n\n### Do\n- DO use React Query for all server state\n- DO write error boundaries around async components\n- DO validate all API inputs with Zod\n- DO use semantic HTML elements\n- DO handle loading and error states for every async operation\n\n### Don''t\n- DON''T use `any` type — ever\n- DON''T install new dependencies without asking\n- DON''T use `useEffect` for data fetching — use React Query\n- DON''T put business logic in components — extract to `/lib`\n- DON''T use inline styles — use Tailwind classes\n\nCustomize these lists with rules specific to your project."}, {"name": "Testing & Quality", "guidelines": "### Testing Framework\n- **Unit/integration**: e.g., Vitest, Jest\n- **E2E**: e.g., Playwright, Cypress\n- **Test file naming**: e.g., `*.test.ts` colocated with source\n\n### What to Test\n- Business logic and utility functions: always\n- API route handlers: always\n- Component rendering: for complex interactive components\n- E2E: critical user flows only\n\n### Quality Gates\n- Linting: ESLint config and key rules\n- Formatting: Prettier/Biome config\n- CI checks: what must pass before merge\n- Type checking: `tsc --noEmit` in CI"}, {"name": "Common Pitfalls", "guidelines": "Things AI assistants commonly get wrong in this codebase. List specific gotchas:\n\n- **Pitfall**: Description of what goes wrong and why\n- **Correct approach**: What to do instead\n- **Pitfall**: Another common mistake\n- **Correct approach**: The right pattern\n\nInclude:\n- Deprecated patterns that still appear in the codebase\n- Environment-specific quirks (e.g., ''Prisma client must be regenerated after schema changes'')\n- Files or modules that need extra care\n- Known bugs or workarounds the AI should be aware of"}]}' WHERE name = 'AI Coding Rules & Standards' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write for the developer consuming the API, not the team that built it. Lead with working code examples in at least two languages. Error responses should include actionable troubleshooting steps. Authentication should have a copy-pasteable quickstart. Organize endpoints by what developers want to accomplish, not just by HTTP method.", "sections": [{"name": "API Overview", "guidelines": "High-level summary of the API.\n\n### Purpose\n- What the API does and the key problems it solves\n- Primary use cases and who the consumers are\n\n### Architecture\n- API style: REST, GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket\n- Base URL and versioning strategy (e.g., `/api/v1/`)\n- Rate limiting and throttling policies\n- Data format: JSON, XML, protobuf\n\n### Core Capabilities\nList the main things developers can do with this API:\n- [Capability 1]: Brief description\n- [Capability 2]: Brief description\n- [Capability 3]: Brief description"}, {"name": "Authentication", "guidelines": "How developers authenticate with the API.\n\n### Methods\nFor each supported method:\n- **Method**: API Key / OAuth 2.0 / Bearer Token / etc.\n- **Setup**: Step-by-step instructions to get credentials\n- **Usage**: How to include credentials in requests (header, query param)\n\n### Quick Start\n```\ncurl -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN\" \\\nhttps://api.example.com/v1/resource\n```\n\n### Token Management\n- How to obtain and refresh tokens\n- Expiration policy\n- Secure storage best practices\n\n### Common Issues\n- [Error]: [Cause and fix]\n- [Error]: [Cause and fix]"}, {"name": "Endpoints", "guidelines": "Detailed reference for every API endpoint.\n\n### Format for Each Endpoint\n```\nMETHOD /path\nDescription: What it does\nAuth: Required / Public\n\nParameters:\n- param (type, required/optional): Description\n\nRequest Body:\n{ field: type, field: type }\n\nResponse (200):\n{ field: type, field: type }\n\nError Responses:\n- 400: Invalid input — [details]\n- 401: Unauthorized\n- 404: Resource not found\n```\n\n### Grouping\nOrganize endpoints by resource or use case (e.g., Users, Projects, Billing), not just by HTTP method.\n\n### Pagination\nDescribe the pagination approach: cursor-based, offset, or page-number. Show request and response format."}, {"name": "Error Handling", "guidelines": "How errors are communicated and how to handle them.\n\n### Error Response Format\n```json\n{\n\"error\": {\n\"code\": \"VALIDATION_ERROR\",\n\"message\": \"Email is required\",\n\"details\": [...]\n}\n}\n```\n\n### Error Code Reference\n| Code | HTTP Status | Description | Resolution |\n|------|-------------|-------------|------------|\n| VALIDATION_ERROR | 400 | Invalid input | Check request body |\n| UNAUTHORIZED | 401 | Missing or invalid token | Re-authenticate |\n| FORBIDDEN | 403 | Insufficient permissions | Check API key scopes |\n| NOT_FOUND | 404 | Resource doesn''t exist | Verify resource ID |\n| RATE_LIMITED | 429 | Too many requests | Back off and retry |\n| INTERNAL_ERROR | 500 | Server error | Retry; contact support if persistent |\n\n### Retry Strategy\n- Which errors are retryable (429, 500, 503)\n- Recommended backoff strategy (exponential with jitter)\n- Maximum retry attempts"}, {"name": "Code Examples", "guidelines": "Working examples in common languages.\n\n### cURL\n```bash\ncurl -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/resource \\\n-H \"Authorization: Bearer TOKEN\" \\\n-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n-d ''{\"name\": \"example\"}''\n```\n\n### JavaScript / TypeScript\n```javascript\nconst response = await fetch(''https://api.example.com/v1/resource'', {\nmethod: ''POST'',\nheaders: {\n''Authorization'': ''Bearer TOKEN'',\n''Content-Type'': ''application/json'',\n},\nbody: JSON.stringify({ name: ''example'' }),\n});\n```\n\n### Python\n```python\nimport requests\nresponse = requests.post(\n''https://api.example.com/v1/resource'',\nheaders={''Authorization'': ''Bearer TOKEN''},\njson={''name'': ''example''},\n)\n```\n\nInclude examples for the most common use cases, not just basic CRUD."}, {"name": "Webhooks & Events", "guidelines": "If the API supports webhooks or event subscriptions.\n\n### Available Events\n- **event.type**: When it fires and what data it includes\n- **event.type**: When it fires and what data it includes\n\n### Webhook Setup\n- How to register a webhook endpoint\n- Payload format and signature verification\n- Retry policy for failed deliveries\n\n### Security\n- How to verify webhook signatures\n- IP allowlisting (if applicable)\n- Handling duplicate events (idempotency)\n\nIf webhooks are not applicable, describe any polling or streaming alternatives."}, {"name": "SDKs, Rate Limits & Support", "guidelines": "Practical information for developers integrating with the API.\n\n### SDKs & Libraries\n- Official SDKs: [Languages and install commands]\n- Community libraries: [Notable third-party options]\n- OpenAPI/Swagger spec URL for code generation\n\n### Rate Limits\n| Tier | Requests/min | Burst | Notes |\n|------|-------------|-------|-------|\n| Free | [limit] | [limit] | |\n| Pro | [limit] | [limit] | |\n| Enterprise | [limit] | [limit] | Custom |\n\n### Support & Resources\n- API status page URL\n- Developer documentation URL\n- Community forum or Discord\n- Support email or ticketing for escalations\n- Changelog / release notes URL"}]}' WHERE name = 'API Documentation' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Be evidence-driven and specific throughout. Every claim should reference data, user research, or market insight. Prioritize crisp problem statements and measurable goals over lengthy narratives. The impact sizing model should include actual calculations with stated assumptions. Key flows should be detailed enough that a designer or engineer could build from them without further clarification.", "sections": [{"name": "Problem", "guidelines": "Describe the problem in 1-2 sentences. Someone should be able to read this and communicate the customer and business value + risks.\n\n### Guidelines\n- State the problem as a hypothesis you can test\n- Highlight evidence or insights that support this\n- Include both the **customer pain** and the **business opportunity**\n- Be specific: ''Users churn after 7 days because they never find [feature]'' not ''engagement is low''"}, {"name": "High Level Approach", "guidelines": "The rough shape of how you might tackle the problem. A few sentences max.\n\n### Guidelines\n- Describe the approach, not the full solution\n- This should communicate direction to the team without over-constraining design\n- Think of it as: ''We''re going to solve this by [approach], roughly by [method]''"}, {"name": "Narrative", "guidelines": "Hypothetical stories that paint a picture of what life looks like for customers today.\n\n### Include\n- **Common use case**: The typical user scenario (80% of usage)\n- **Edge case**: An unusual but important scenario that tests the solution\n- **Before/after**: What life looks like without your solution vs. with it\n\nStories should create empathy and help the team internalize user needs. Make them vivid and specific."}, {"name": "Goals", "guidelines": "High-level goals, ideally in priority order.\n\n### Measurable Goals\nGoals with concrete metrics:\n- Goal 1: [Metric] from [baseline] to [target]\n- Goal 2: [Metric] from [baseline] to [target]\n\n### Immeasurable Goals\nGoals about how the experience should *feel*:\n- Users should feel [confident/delighted/empowered] when [doing action]\n- The experience should feel [fast/simple/professional]\n\nKeep it short — 3-5 goals max, in priority order."}, {"name": "Metrics", "guidelines": "Specific, operationalized metrics to track success.\n\n### North Star Metric\nThe single most important metric:\n- **Metric**: [Name]\n- **Current baseline**: [Value]\n- **Target**: [Value]\n- **Measurement**: How and where this is tracked\n\n### Secondary Metrics\nSupporting metrics that indicate progress:\n- [Metric 2]: [Baseline] → [Target]\n- [Metric 3]: [Baseline] → [Target]\n\n### Guardrail Metrics\nMetrics that must NOT degrade:\n- [Guardrail metric]: Must stay above [threshold]\n- State what amount of movement would be **unacceptable** and trigger a rollback"}, {"name": "Impact Sizing Model", "guidelines": "A quantitative model estimating the impact on key metrics.\n\n### Calculation\nShow your work step by step:\n1. **[Input assumption]**: [Number] — source: [where this comes from]\n2. **[Input assumption]**: [Number] — source: [where this comes from]\n3. **[Calculation step]**: [Formula] = [Result]\n4. **[Final estimate]**: Expected impact on north star metric\n\n### Revenue/Bottom-Line Impact\n- Estimated impact on revenue or profit\n- Confidence level: Low / Medium / High\n- Key assumptions that could change the estimate"}, {"name": "Non-goals", "guidelines": "Explicit areas you do NOT plan to address.\n\n### Format\nFor each non-goal:\n- **Not doing**: [What you''re skipping]\n- **Why**: [Rationale — too expensive, wrong timing, different team, low impact]\n\n### Guidelines\n- Non-goals are as important as goals — they prevent scope creep\n- If someone might reasonably assume it''s in scope, call it out as a non-goal\n- Include things you considered but explicitly decided against"}, {"name": "Solution Alignment", "guidelines": "Draw the perimeter of the solution. Do not force others to identify scope.\n\n### What''s In\n- Core experience that MUST be built\n- Quality bar for the initial release\n\n### What''s Out\n- Features saved for later\n- Quality tradeoffs you''re accepting for now\n\n### Open Questions\n- Decisions that still need input from design/engineering\n- Areas where you''re deliberately leaving room for the team to explore"}, {"name": "Key Features: Plan of Record", "guidelines": "The features that shape the solution, in priority order.\n\n### Format\nFor each feature:\n- **Feature**: [Name]\n- **Priority**: P0 (must ship) / P1 (should ship) / P2 (stretch)\n- **Description**: What it does and why it matters\n- **Size estimate**: Small / Medium / Large\n\n### Guidelines\n- Think of this as drawing the perimeter of the solution space\n- Challenge each feature: can a smaller version be shipped independently?\n- The team fills in the details — you define the boundaries"}, {"name": "Future Considerations", "guidelines": "Features you are saving for later that might inform how you build now.\n\n### Planned Follow-ups\n- **Feature**: [Description] — Planned for [timeframe/phase]\n- **Feature**: [Description] — Planned for [timeframe/phase]\n\n### Build-Now Implications\nAnything about these future features that should influence current architecture or design decisions. Example: ''We''ll add multi-language support in Q3, so don''t hard-code strings.''"}, {"name": "Key Flows", "guidelines": "End-to-end experience shown screen by screen. Get detailed here — not high level.\n\n### Format\n**Flow: [Name]**\n1. **Screen**: [Page/view name]\n- **User sees**: [What''s on screen]\n- **User does**: [Action they take]\n- **Result**: [What happens next]\n2. **Screen**: [Next page/view]\n- ...\n\n### Guidelines\n- Walk through every click, not just the high-level steps\n- Include empty states, loading states, and error states\n- An engineer should be able to build from this without asking questions"}, {"name": "Key Logic", "guidelines": "Business rules that guide design and development.\n\n### Rules\nFor each rule:\n- **Rule**: [Clear statement of the logic]\n- **Applies when**: [Conditions]\n- **Example**: [Concrete scenario]\n\n### Edge Cases\n- What happens when [boundary condition]?\n- What happens when [unusual input]?\n- What happens when [concurrent action]?\n\nMake it so an engineer feels everything is thought through."}, {"name": "Launch Plan", "guidelines": "How this product gets to market.\n\n### Phases\nFor each phase:\n- **Phase**: [Name] — [Purpose]\n- **Entry criteria**: What must be true to start this phase\n- **Exit criteria**: What must be true to move to the next phase\n- **Duration**: Expected timeline\n\n### Experiment Design (if applicable)\n- **Type**: A/B test, staged rollout, beta program\n- **Sample size**: Users needed for statistical significance\n- **Duration**: Time needed based on north star metric goal\n- **Success criteria**: What metric movement triggers full launch\n\n### Risks & Dependencies\n- Risks that can throw a wrench in timelines\n- Dependencies on other teams or external factors\n- Contingency plans for each"}, {"name": "Key Milestones", "guidelines": "The critical checkpoints from now to launch.\n\n### Format\n| Milestone | Date | Owner | Status |\n|-----------|------|-------|--------|\n| Design complete | [Date] | [Name] | [Status] |\n| Engineering kickoff | [Date] | [Name] | [Status] |\n| Internal dogfood | [Date] | [Name] | [Status] |\n| Beta launch | [Date] | [Name] | [Status] |\n| GA launch | [Date] | [Name] | [Status] |\n\n### Dependencies\nCall out any milestone that blocks another or depends on an external team."}]}' WHERE name = 'Aakash''s PRD' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Reference specific WCAG 2.1 AA standards for each checklist item. Include practical testing methods (manual vs. automated) for each criterion. Organize by implementation priority so teams can tackle high-impact items first. Include code-level guidance where relevant (ARIA attributes, semantic HTML). Flag items that are commonly overlooked.", "sections": [{"name": "Accessibility Overview", "guidelines": "Scope and goals for this accessibility assessment.\n\n### Target Standard\n- **WCAG Level**: 2.1 AA (recommended minimum) / 2.1 AAA / Section 508\n- **Legal requirements**: ADA, EAA (European), AODA (Canadian), or other\n\n### Scope\n- Which pages, features, or flows are covered\n- Which user types: visual, auditory, motor, cognitive impairments\n- Which devices: desktop, mobile, tablet, screen reader\n\n### Tools\n- Automated: axe, Lighthouse, WAVE\n- Manual: Screen reader testing (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS)\n- User testing with people who use assistive technology"}, {"name": "Perceivable", "guidelines": "Can all users perceive the content? (WCAG Principle 1)\n\n### Images & Non-Text Content\n- [ ] All images have descriptive `alt` text (not ''image'' or ''photo'')\n- [ ] Decorative images use `alt=\"\"` or CSS background\n- [ ] Complex images (charts, diagrams) have extended descriptions\n- [ ] Icons with meaning have accessible labels\n\n### Video & Audio\n- [ ] Videos have captions (not auto-generated without review)\n- [ ] Audio content has transcripts\n- [ ] No content relies solely on audio or visual cues\n\n### Color & Contrast\n- [ ] Text contrast meets 4.5:1 ratio (normal text) and 3:1 (large text)\n- [ ] Information is never conveyed by color alone\n- [ ] UI components have 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors\n- [ ] Focus indicators are clearly visible\n\n### Responsive & Adaptable\n- [ ] Content is usable at 200% zoom\n- [ ] Content reflows at 320px width without horizontal scroll\n- [ ] Reading order is correct when CSS is disabled"}, {"name": "Operable", "guidelines": "Can all users operate the interface? (WCAG Principle 2)\n\n### Keyboard\n- [ ] All functionality is available via keyboard alone\n- [ ] Tab order follows a logical sequence\n- [ ] Focus is never trapped (except intentional modals with escape)\n- [ ] Skip-to-content link is present and functional\n- [ ] Custom components (dropdowns, modals, tabs) have proper keyboard support\n\n### Navigation\n- [ ] Page has descriptive `<title>`\n- [ ] Headings follow a logical hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3, no skips)\n- [ ] Multiple ways to find content (nav, search, sitemap)\n- [ ] Breadcrumbs for multi-level navigation\n\n### Timing & Motion\n- [ ] No time limits, or users can extend/disable them\n- [ ] Auto-playing content can be paused or stopped\n- [ ] Animations respect `prefers-reduced-motion`\n- [ ] No content flashes more than 3 times per second\n\n### Touch & Pointer\n- [ ] Touch targets are at least 44x44px\n- [ ] Gestures have single-pointer alternatives\n- [ ] Drag-and-drop has keyboard alternatives"}, {"name": "Understandable", "guidelines": "Can all users understand the content and interface? (WCAG Principle 3)\n\n### Language & Readability\n- [ ] Page language is set with `lang` attribute\n- [ ] Content is written at an appropriate reading level\n- [ ] Abbreviations and jargon are explained on first use\n- [ ] Consistent terminology throughout the product\n\n### Predictability\n- [ ] Navigation is consistent across pages\n- [ ] Components behave consistently (same icon = same action)\n- [ ] No unexpected context changes on focus or input\n\n### Forms & Input\n- [ ] All form fields have visible labels (not just placeholders)\n- [ ] Required fields are clearly indicated\n- [ ] Error messages are specific: ''Email must include @'' not ''Invalid input''\n- [ ] Errors are associated with the field using `aria-describedby`\n- [ ] Success and error states are announced to screen readers\n- [ ] Form instructions appear before the form, not just after\n\n### Error Prevention\n- [ ] Destructive actions require confirmation\n- [ ] Users can review before submitting important data\n- [ ] Undo is available where practical"}, {"name": "Robust", "guidelines": "Does the product work across assistive technologies? (WCAG Principle 4)\n\n### Semantic HTML\n- [ ] Proper use of landmarks: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`\n- [ ] Interactive elements use correct roles (`<button>`, `<a>`, not `<div onClick>`)\n- [ ] Lists use `<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<dl>` appropriately\n- [ ] Tables have `<th>`, `scope`, and `<caption>` where needed\n\n### ARIA Usage\n- [ ] ARIA is used only when native HTML is insufficient\n- [ ] `aria-label` and `aria-labelledby` are used correctly\n- [ ] `aria-live` regions announce dynamic content changes\n- [ ] Custom widgets follow ARIA authoring practices (combobox, dialog, tabs, etc.)\n\n### Screen Reader Testing\n- [ ] Tested with VoiceOver (macOS/iOS)\n- [ ] Tested with NVDA or JAWS (Windows)\n- [ ] Tested with TalkBack (Android)\n- [ ] All interactive elements are announced with correct name, role, and state"}, {"name": "Findings & Remediation Plan", "guidelines": "Issues found and the plan to fix them.\n\n### Issues\nFor each issue:\n- **Issue**: [Description]\n- **WCAG criterion**: [e.g., 1.4.3 Contrast]\n- **Severity**: Critical / Major / Minor\n- **Location**: [Page/component]\n- **Current behavior**: [What happens now]\n- **Expected behavior**: [What should happen]\n- **Fix**: [Specific code change or design update]\n\n### Priority Matrix\n| Severity | Count | Fix By |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| Critical | [#] | Before launch |\n| Major | [#] | Within 30 days |\n| Minor | [#] | Within 90 days |\n\n### Process Improvements\n- Add accessibility to the design review checklist\n- Add automated accessibility tests to CI pipeline\n- Schedule regular manual audits (quarterly recommended)\n- Include accessibility in the definition of done\n\n### Resources\n- WCAG quick reference: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/quickref/\n- ARIA authoring practices: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/\n- Testing tools: axe DevTools, Lighthouse, WAVE"}]}' WHERE name = 'Accessibility Compliance Checklist' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a technical blueprint that an AI coding assistant or engineer could use to scaffold the entire application. Be opinionated about technology choices — pick specific tools and explain why. Data models should list actual fields with types. API routes should specify methods, paths, and payloads. Auth should name the exact provider and flow. Keep it practical and buildable, not theoretical.", "sections": [{"name": "System Overview", "guidelines": "What the app does in 2-3 sentences, who it is for, and the high-level architecture.\n\n### Architecture Pattern\n- **Type**: Monolith / Microservices / Serverless / Jamstack\n- **Key components**: Frontend, Backend API, Database, Background Jobs, External Services\n\n### System Diagram\nDescribe how the major pieces connect: which services talk to which, what protocols they use (REST, GraphQL, WebSocket), and where data flows."}, {"name": "Data Model", "guidelines": "Define every core entity with its fields, types, and relationships.\n\n### Entity Format\n```\nUser\n- id: uuid (PK)\n- email: text (unique)\n- name: text\n- role: enum (admin, member)\n- createdAt: timestamptz\n```\n\n### Relationships\n- **User → Project**: One-to-many (user has many projects)\n- **Project → Organization**: Many-to-one (project belongs to org)\n- **User ↔ Organization**: Many-to-many (via Membership join table)\n\nNote which fields are indexed and any unique constraints beyond primary keys."}, {"name": "API Design", "guidelines": "List every API route grouped by resource.\n\n### Format\n```\nMETHOD /api/path\nAuth: required | public\nBody: { field: type, ... }\nResponse: { field: type, ... }\n```\n\n### Example\n```\nPOST /api/projects\nAuth: required\nBody: { name: string, description?: string }\nResponse: { id, name, description, createdAt }\n\nGET /api/projects/:id\nAuth: required\nResponse: { id, name, description, members: [...] }\n```\n\nNote rate limiting, pagination approach, and error response format."}, {"name": "Auth & Permissions", "guidelines": "### Authentication\n- **Provider**: e.g., Clerk, NextAuth, Supabase Auth\n- **Methods**: Magic link, OAuth (Google, GitHub), email/password\n- **Session handling**: JWT tokens, cookie sessions, etc.\n\n### Authorization\n- **Roles**: List each role and its capabilities\n- **Enforcement**: How auth is checked (middleware, per-route, RLS)\n- **Public routes**: Which pages/endpoints don''t require auth\n\n### Multi-tenancy\nIf applicable: how organizations/workspaces are scoped and how data isolation works."}, {"name": "Third-Party Services", "guidelines": "List every external service the app depends on.\n\n### Format\nFor each service:\n- **Service**: What it does\n- **SDK/Library**: Which package to use\n- **Config**: Key environment variables and setup\n- **Failure handling**: What happens when this service is down\n\n### Common Services\n- **Payments**: Stripe, Lemon Squeezy, etc.\n- **Email**: Resend, SendGrid, Postmark\n- **File storage**: S3, Cloudflare R2, Uploadthing\n- **AI/ML**: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replicate\n- **Analytics**: PostHog, Mixpanel, Segment\n- **Monitoring**: Sentry, LogRocket"}, {"name": "Frontend Architecture", "guidelines": "### Tech Choices\n- **Framework**: Next.js, Vite + React, SvelteKit, etc.\n- **Component library**: shadcn/ui, Radix, Material UI, etc.\n- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS, CSS Modules, styled-components\n- **State management**: React Query, Zustand, Redux, etc.\n\n### Page Structure\nList every page/route with its path and purpose:\n- `/` — Landing or dashboard\n- `/app` — Main application\n- `/settings` — User/org settings\n\n### Data Fetching Strategy\n- Server components vs. client components\n- Caching and revalidation approach\n- Global loading and error state handling"}, {"name": "Infrastructure & Deployment", "guidelines": "### Hosting\n- **Frontend**: Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, etc.\n- **Backend**: Same as frontend / Railway / Fly.io / AWS\n- **Database**: Neon, Supabase, PlanetScale, RDS\n\n### CI/CD Pipeline\n- Build and test steps\n- Preview deployments for PRs\n- Production deployment trigger (merge to main, manual, etc.)\n\n### Environments\n- **Development**: Local setup, seed data\n- **Staging**: Config and purpose\n- **Production**: Config, monitoring, alerts\n\n### Environment Variables\nList all required env vars by category (auth, database, services, feature flags). Note which are secret vs. public."}]}' WHERE name = 'App Architecture Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this like an engineering investigation report — factual, precise, and evidence-based. Reproduction steps should be exact (click this, type that, see this error). Root cause analysis should trace the actual code path that fails, not speculate. The proposed fix should be specific enough for an AI coding assistant or engineer to implement directly. Include what NOT to do if there are tempting but wrong fixes. The test plan should verify the fix works and confirm nothing else broke.", "sections": [{"name": "Bug Summary", "guidelines": "### Overview\nWhat is happening in 1-2 sentences — describe the broken behavior, not the fix.\n\n### Metadata\n- **Severity**: Critical / High / Medium / Low\n- **Affected users**: Percentage or count, and which segments\n- **First observed**: Date and how it was discovered\n- **Environment**: Production / Staging / Both\n- **Error tracking**: Link to Sentry issue, LogRocket session, or relevant logs\n\n### Business Impact\nWhat is the cost of this bug remaining unfixed? Revenue loss, blocked users, trust erosion, support load, etc."}, {"name": "Reproduction Steps", "guidelines": "Exact numbered steps to reproduce, starting from a clean state.\n\n### Steps\n1. Go to [URL/page]\n2. Log in as [role/account type]\n3. Click [element] / Enter [input]\n4. Observe [broken behavior]\n\n### Environment Details\n- **Browser/device**: Chrome 120 on macOS, Safari on iOS 17, etc.\n- **Account type**: Admin, free user, new signup, etc.\n- **Required data**: Specific data conditions needed to trigger the bug\n- **Frequency**: Always reproducible / Intermittent (describe conditions that increase likelihood)\n- **Timing**: Any time-dependent or sequence-dependent factors"}, {"name": "Expected vs. Actual Behavior", "guidelines": "### Expected Behavior\nWhat SHOULD happen according to the spec, design, or previous working state. Reference the source of truth (Figma, PRD, previous release).\n\n### Actual Behavior\nWhat ACTUALLY happens. Be precise:\n- **Visual**: What the user sees (include screenshots)\n- **Console**: Any errors in browser console or server logs\n- **Network**: Failed requests, unexpected responses, status codes\n- **Data**: Any data corruption, missing records, or incorrect state\n\n### What Still Works\nNote any related functionality that is NOT broken — this helps narrow the root cause."}, {"name": "Root Cause Analysis", "guidelines": "### Investigation Trail\nWhat was checked and ruled out during debugging:\n1. Checked [hypothesis A] → Ruled out because [evidence]\n2. Checked [hypothesis B] → Found the issue here\n\n### Root Cause\n- **File**: Exact file path and function/line\n- **What happens**: Trace the code path from user action to failure\n- **Why it fails**: The specific logic error, missing check, race condition, or data issue\n- **When it was introduced**: Regression from [PR/commit]? Always existed? Data-dependent?\n\n### Contributing Factors\nAnything that made this bug possible: missing tests, unclear spec, tech debt, etc."}, {"name": "Proposed Fix", "guidelines": "### Recommended Approach\n- **Files to modify**: List each file and the change needed\n- **Logic change**: Describe the fix at a code-concept level\n- **Why this is correct**: Explain why this fix addresses the root cause\n\n### Alternative Approaches\nIf there are other ways to fix this:\n- **Option B**: Description, with pros/cons vs. recommended approach\n- **Option C**: Description, with pros/cons\n\n### Risks\n- Could this fix break anything else?\n- Any performance implications?\n- Does this need a database migration or data backfill?"}, {"name": "Test Plan", "guidelines": "### Manual Verification\n1. Follow reproduction steps → Bug no longer occurs\n2. [Additional manual check]\n3. [Edge case verification]\n\n### Automated Tests to Write\n- **Unit test**: Test the specific function/logic that was fixed\n- **Integration test**: Test the API endpoint or data flow end-to-end\n- **E2E test**: Test the user flow if this is a critical path\n\n### Regression Checks\n- Verify [related feature A] still works correctly\n- Verify [related feature B] still works correctly\n- Monitor error tracking (Sentry) for 24-48 hours post-deploy"}, {"name": "Prevention", "guidelines": "How to prevent this class of bug from recurring.\n\n### Immediate Actions\n- Add specific test coverage: [describe exact test]\n- Add input validation: [describe exact check]\n- Add monitoring/alerting: [describe exact alert]\n\n### Systemic Improvements\n- Type safety: [specific TypeScript strictness or type guard]\n- Code review checklist: [specific item to add]\n- Architecture change: [if applicable, describe structural fix]\n\n### Documentation\n- Update relevant docs or runbooks\n- Add to known issues or team knowledge base"}]}' WHERE name = 'Bug Investigation & Fix Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Go beyond surface-level feature comparison. Include specific pricing, positioning gaps, and strategic differences. Be honest about your own product''s weaknesses relative to competitors. Customer sentiment should come from real reviews and data sources. Every insight should connect to an actionable product or strategy recommendation.", "sections": [{"name": "Company Profiles", "guidelines": "Background on each competitor being analyzed.\n\n### Format for Each Competitor\n**[Competitor Name]**\n- **Overview**: What they do, company size, funding stage, headquarters\n- **Founded**: Year and brief history\n- **Revenue/Funding**: Publicly available financial signals\n- **Key products**: Their main offerings and target market\n- **Leadership**: Notable executives and their background\n- **Recent moves**: Acquisitions, pivots, major launches in the last 12 months\n\n### Your Company (for comparison)\nBrief summary of your own positioning to anchor the analysis."}, {"name": "Product Comparison", "guidelines": "Feature-by-feature analysis against your product.\n\n### Feature Matrix\n| Feature | Your Product | Competitor A | Competitor B | Competitor C |\n|---------|-------------|-------------|-------------|-------------|\n| [Feature 1] | ✓ / ✗ / Partial | ✓ / ✗ / Partial | ✓ / ✗ / Partial | ✓ / ✗ / Partial |\n| [Feature 2] | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\n### Deep Dives\nFor the 3-5 most important features, compare in detail:\n- **Your approach**: How you do it\n- **Their approach**: How they do it\n- **User impact**: Which approach is better for users and why\n\n### Innovation & Technology\n- Unique capabilities each competitor has\n- Technology investments (AI/ML, platform, infrastructure)\n- R&D signals (hiring, patents, open source)"}, {"name": "SWOT Analysis", "guidelines": "Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats for each competitor.\n\n### Format for Each Competitor\n**[Competitor Name]**\n\n| | Helpful | Harmful |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| **Internal** | **Strengths**: [List] | **Weaknesses**: [List] |\n| **External** | **Opportunities**: [List] | **Threats**: [List] |\n\n### Your SWOT (Honest Assessment)\nApply the same framework to your own product — be candid about weaknesses.\n\n### Key Insights\n- Where competitors are strongest relative to you\n- Where you have clear advantages\n- Where the market has unmet needs that nobody serves well"}, {"name": "Market Positioning & Pricing", "guidelines": "How competitors position themselves and price their products.\n\n### Positioning Map\nDescribe where each competitor sits along key dimensions:\n- **Axis 1**: e.g., Simple ←→ Enterprise-grade\n- **Axis 2**: e.g., Affordable ←→ Premium\n\n### Brand Perception\nFor each competitor:\n- How the market perceives them (trusted, innovative, cheap, complex)\n- Key messaging themes from their website and marketing\n- Customer sentiment from reviews (G2, Capterra, Reddit, Twitter)\n\n### Pricing Comparison\n| | Free Tier | Entry Price | Mid Tier | Enterprise |\n|---|-----------|-------------|----------|------------|\n| Your Product | [details] | [price] | [price] | [price] |\n| Competitor A | [details] | [price] | [price] | [price] |\n\n### Customer Acquisition\n- Primary channels each competitor uses (PLG, sales-led, partnerships)\n- Estimated marketing spend or presence\n- Retention strategies: loyalty programs, lock-in, switching costs"}, {"name": "Strategic Insights", "guidelines": "Actionable conclusions that should inform your strategy.\n\n### Key Takeaways\n3-5 most important insights from the analysis:\n1. **Insight**: [Finding] → **Implication**: [What to do about it]\n2. **Insight**: [Finding] → **Implication**: [What to do about it]\n\n### Competitive Advantages to Protect\n- Your strongest differentiators that competitors would struggle to replicate\n- How to strengthen and communicate these advantages\n\n### Vulnerabilities to Address\n- Areas where competitors clearly outperform you\n- Specific actions to close the gap, with priority and timeline\n\n### Market Gaps\n- Unmet needs that no competitor serves well\n- Opportunity to differentiate or create a new category\n\n### Competitive Response Plan\n- How to respond if [Competitor A] launches [specific feature]\n- How to respond if [Competitor B] undercuts on price\n- Signals to monitor (job postings, funding, product updates)"}]}' WHERE name = 'Competitive Analysis Report' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Map the actual customer experience, not the idealized version. Include emotional states at each stage — frustration, confusion, delight. Touchpoints should be specific (e.g., \"order confirmation email\" not \"communication\"). Opportunities for improvement should connect directly to observed pain points with clear prioritization. Be honest about where the experience breaks down.", "sections": [{"name": "Customer Persona", "guidelines": "A detailed profile of the primary customer whose journey you are mapping.\n\n### Demographics\n- Age, role, location, income level, company size\n\n### Behavior\n- Buying habits, brand preferences, digital behavior, tool usage\n\n### Needs & Goals\n- What they are trying to accomplish\n- What success looks like for them\n\n### Challenges\n- Pain points and frustrations they face daily\n- What prevents them from reaching their goals\n\n### Preferred Channels\n- How they prefer to communicate: email, chat, phone, self-serve\n- Where they spend time: social media, communities, publications"}, {"name": "Journey Stages", "guidelines": "The customer''s experience mapped across each stage of their relationship with your product.\n\n### Awareness\n- **Actions**: How they first discover your product\n- **Feelings**: Curiosity, skepticism, excitement\n- **Touchpoints**: Ads, content, word of mouth, social media\n- **Key question**: ''Is this relevant to me?''\n\n### Consideration\n- **Actions**: Comparing you against alternatives\n- **Feelings**: Evaluation, uncertainty, hope\n- **Touchpoints**: Website, demos, reviews, free trial\n- **Key question**: ''Is this better than what I have?''\n\n### Decision\n- **Actions**: Making the purchase/signup decision\n- **Feelings**: Confidence or hesitation\n- **Touchpoints**: Pricing page, sales call, checkout flow\n- **Key question**: ''Is this worth the investment?''\n\n### Onboarding & First Value\n- **Actions**: Setting up, learning, first success\n- **Feelings**: Excitement, confusion, accomplishment\n- **Touchpoints**: Onboarding flow, docs, support, email sequences\n- **Key question**: ''Can I get value quickly?''\n\n### Retention\n- **Actions**: Regular usage, deeper adoption\n- **Feelings**: Satisfaction, habit formation, or frustration\n- **Touchpoints**: Product UI, feature updates, support\n- **Key question**: ''Is this still worth it?''\n\n### Advocacy\n- **Actions**: Recommending to others, writing reviews\n- **Feelings**: Pride, loyalty, community belonging\n- **Touchpoints**: Referral programs, communities, social sharing\n- **Key question**: ''Am I proud to recommend this?''"}, {"name": "Pain Points & Moments of Truth", "guidelines": "Specific points where the experience breaks down or delights.\n\n### Pain Points\nFor each pain point:\n- **Stage**: Which journey stage\n- **Pain**: What goes wrong\n- **Severity**: Frustrating / Painful / Causes churn\n- **Frequency**: How often it occurs\n- **Evidence**: Data, quotes, or support tickets that prove this\n\n### Moments of Truth\nCritical interactions that disproportionately influence the customer''s perception:\n- **First impression**: The initial experience that sets expectations\n- **Aha moment**: When the customer first realizes the value\n- **Recovery moment**: How you handle things when they go wrong\n\nThese are the highest-leverage points to improve."}, {"name": "Opportunities for Improvement", "guidelines": "Gaps and possibilities identified from mapping the journey.\n\n### Unmet Needs\n- Needs the product doesn''t address yet\n- Features competitors offer that you don''t\n- Workarounds customers have built\n\n### Experience Gaps\n- Stages where the experience is weakest\n- Handoffs between channels that feel broken\n- Information customers need but can''t easily find\n\n### Innovation Areas\n- Where introducing new features or processes could transform the experience\n- Technology-enabled improvements (AI, automation, personalization)\n- Areas where simplification would have outsized impact\n\n### Quick Wins\n- Low-effort, high-impact improvements that can ship fast"}, {"name": "Action Plan", "guidelines": "Concrete initiatives to improve the journey, organized by priority.\n\n### Immediate (This Quarter)\nFor each initiative:\n- **Initiative**: [Name]\n- **Journey stage**: [Which stage it improves]\n- **Expected impact**: [Which pain point it addresses and metric it moves]\n- **Owner**: [Who is responsible]\n- **Effort**: Small / Medium / Large\n\n### Near-Term (Next 2 Quarters)\n- Larger initiatives requiring design and engineering investment\n- Pilot programs to test before full rollout\n\n### Long-Term (6-12 Months)\n- Structural changes to the product or organization\n- Platform or infrastructure investments\n\n### Success Metrics\n- How you will measure whether the journey is actually improving\n- Baseline measurements to take now\n- Review cadence and accountability"}]}' WHERE name = 'Customer Journey Map' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a precise technical reference for building the database. Entity definitions should list actual field names with types (e.g., id: uuid, email: text, createdAt: timestamptz). Relationships should be explicit about cardinality and foreign keys. Access patterns should describe real queries the app will run. Be opinionated about index choices and explain the tradeoffs. Use the user''s stated database technology (Postgres, MySQL, MongoDB, etc.).", "sections": [{"name": "Overview & Goals", "guidelines": "What data this system manages and the key design priorities.\n\n### Database Choice\n- **Technology**: PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, SQLite, etc.\n- **Hosting**: Neon, Supabase, PlanetScale, self-hosted, etc.\n- **Rationale**: Why this database fits the use case\n\n### Design Priorities\n- **Read vs. write heavy**: Which matters more?\n- **Consistency model**: Strong consistency vs. eventual\n- **Normalization**: Normalized, denormalized, or hybrid\n- **Scale expectations**: Expected row counts and query volume"}, {"name": "Core Entities", "guidelines": "Define every table or collection with all fields.\n\n### Format\n```\nTableName\n- id: uuid (PK, default: gen_random_uuid())\n- fieldName: type (constraints)\n- fieldName: type (nullable, default: value)\n- createdAt: timestamptz (default: now())\n- updatedAt: timestamptz\n```\n\n### Field Annotations\n- **PK**: Primary key\n- **FK**: Foreign key (reference: Table.field)\n- **UQ**: Unique constraint\n- **IDX**: Indexed\n- Mark required vs. nullable fields explicitly\n\nGroup related entities together (e.g., User entities, Content entities, Billing entities)."}, {"name": "Relationships & Constraints", "guidelines": "### Relationship Map\nList every relationship between entities:\n- **One-to-one**: User → UserProfile\n- **One-to-many**: User → Post (user has many posts)\n- **Many-to-many**: User ↔ Organization (via Membership table)\n\n### Foreign Key Behavior\nFor each FK, specify:\n- **ON DELETE**: CASCADE, SET NULL, RESTRICT, or NO ACTION\n- **ON UPDATE**: CASCADE or NO ACTION\n\n### Constraints\n- **Unique constraints**: e.g., (orgId, email) must be unique together\n- **Check constraints**: e.g., price > 0, status IN (''active'', ''archived'')\n- **Business rules**: Any logic enforced at the DB level"}, {"name": "Indexes & Performance", "guidelines": "### Required Indexes\nFor each index beyond primary keys:\n- **Table.column(s)**: Index type, which query it supports\n\n### Example\n```\nPost.authorId — B-tree, for filtering posts by author\nPost.(status, createdAt) — Composite, for listing active posts sorted by date\nPost.title — GIN (trigram), for full-text search\n```\n\n### Performance Considerations\n- Queries expected to be slow or complex\n- N+1 risks and how to prevent them (eager loading, joins)\n- Pagination strategy (cursor-based vs. offset)\n- Caching approach for frequently read data"}, {"name": "Access Patterns", "guidelines": "Describe the most common queries the application will run.\n\n### Format\n```\nPattern: List all projects for a user\nQuery: SELECT * FROM Project WHERE ownerId = ? ORDER BY createdAt DESC\nFrequency: Very high (every page load)\nIndexes used: Project.ownerId\n```\n\n### Categories\n- **High-frequency reads**: Dashboard, lists, lookups\n- **Writes**: Creates, updates, deletes\n- **Complex queries**: Aggregations, reports, search\n- **Background jobs**: Scheduled queries, batch processing"}, {"name": "Migration Plan", "guidelines": "### Migration Tool\n- **Tool**: Prisma Migrate, Drizzle Kit, Knex, raw SQL, etc.\n- **Workflow**: How migrations are created, reviewed, and applied\n\n### Seed Data\n- Development seed data for testing\n- Required production seed data (roles, default settings, etc.)\n\n### Evolution Strategy\n- Approach to zero-downtime migrations in production\n- Column rename/removal process (add new → migrate data → remove old)\n- Data backfill procedures for new required columns"}, {"name": "Security & Privacy", "guidelines": "### Sensitive Data Inventory\nIdentify every field containing sensitive data:\n- **PII**: email, name, phone, address\n- **Credentials**: password hashes, API keys, tokens\n- **Financial**: payment info, billing addresses\n\n### Protection Measures\n- **Encryption at rest**: Database-level or column-level\n- **Hashing**: Which fields (e.g., passwords with bcrypt/argon2)\n- **Row-level security**: If applicable, describe RLS policies\n\n### Compliance\n- **Data retention**: How long data is kept, auto-deletion policies\n- **Right to deletion**: Process for GDPR/CCPA delete requests\n- **Audit logging**: Which operations are logged"}]}' WHERE name = 'Database Schema Design' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a tight, actionable spec for one feature that an AI coding assistant can execute in a single session. Be extremely specific about acceptance criteria — each one should be independently testable. The technical approach should name exact files, functions, and components to create or modify. Edge cases should cover error states, empty states, loading states, and permission boundaries. Skip philosophy and context-setting — go straight to what needs to be built.", "sections": [{"name": "Feature Summary", "guidelines": "What this feature does in 1-2 sentences, why it matters, and who uses it.\n\n### Scope\n- **In scope**: What is part of this feature\n- **Out of scope**: What is explicitly NOT included\n- **Prerequisites**: Features or infrastructure that must exist first\n- **Depends on**: APIs, data, or services this feature needs"}, {"name": "Acceptance Criteria", "guidelines": "Numbered list of specific, testable criteria. Each should be independently verifiable.\n\n### Must-Have (P0)\n1. User can [action] and sees [result]\n2. System [validates/enforces] [rule]\n3. [State change] persists after page refresh\n\n### Should-Have (P1)\n4. [Secondary capability]\n5. [Edge case handling]\n\n### Nice-to-Have (P2)\n6. [Polish item]\n\nEach criterion should be a concrete behavior, not a vague quality (''User can filter by date range and sees results update within 500ms'' not ''Filtering works well'')."}, {"name": "User Flow", "guidelines": "Step-by-step walkthrough of the user interaction.\n\n### Happy Path\n1. **Entry point**: User navigates to [page] and sees [initial state]\n2. **Action**: User clicks [element] / fills [form] / selects [option]\n3. **Feedback**: System shows [loading state / optimistic update]\n4. **Result**: User sees [success state / new content / confirmation]\n5. **Next step**: User can now [follow-up action]\n\n### Alternate Paths\n- **Path B**: If user [alternative action], then [what happens]\n- **Path C**: If user [cancels/goes back], then [what happens]\n\nNote specific UI components and layout at each step."}, {"name": "Edge Cases & Error States", "guidelines": "Everything that could go wrong or deviate from the happy path.\n\n### Input Validation\n- Empty/missing required fields → [error message]\n- Invalid format (email, URL, etc.) → [error message]\n- Input too long/short → [error message]\n\n### System Errors\n- Network failure → [retry behavior, offline message]\n- Server error (500) → [error UI, retry option]\n- Timeout → [timeout message, recovery]\n\n### Permission & State\n- Unauthorized access → [redirect, error message]\n- Stale data / concurrent edit → [conflict resolution]\n- Empty state (no data yet) → [empty state UI, CTA]\n- Rate limited → [throttle message]"}, {"name": "Technical Approach", "guidelines": "Implementation strategy — specific enough to build from, but not actual code.\n\n### Files to Create/Modify\n- `src/components/[Feature]/` — New component(s)\n- `src/app/api/[route]/` — New or modified API endpoint\n- `src/lib/[module].ts` — Business logic\n- `prisma/schema.prisma` — Schema changes (if any)\n\n### Data Flow\n1. UI triggers [action]\n2. Calls [API endpoint / server action]\n3. Validates input with [schema]\n4. Reads/writes [database table]\n5. Returns [response shape]\n6. UI updates [state / cache]\n\n### New Dependencies\nList any new packages needed with justification."}, {"name": "Dependencies & Risks", "guidelines": "### Dependencies\n- **Upstream**: APIs or features this depends on\n- **Downstream**: What depends on this feature\n- **External**: Third-party services required\n\n### Technical Risks\n- **Performance**: Will this be slow at scale? What is the expected query volume?\n- **Security**: Any new attack surface (user input, file upload, etc.)?\n- **Data integrity**: Risk of data corruption or loss?\n\n### Rollout Strategy\n- **Feature flag**: Is one needed?\n- **Staged rollout**: Internal → beta → GA?\n- **Rollback plan**: How to revert if something goes wrong"}, {"name": "Test Plan", "guidelines": "### Manual Testing\nStep-by-step QA checklist:\n1. [Test the happy path end to end]\n2. [Test each edge case from above]\n3. [Test on mobile / different browsers if relevant]\n\n### Automated Tests\n- **Unit tests**: Business logic, validation, utilities\n- **Integration tests**: API endpoints with database\n- **E2E tests**: Critical user flow (if warranted)\n\n### Regression Checks\n- Verify [related feature A] still works\n- Verify [related feature B] still works\n- Check error tracking (Sentry) for new errors after deploy"}]}' WHERE name = 'Feature Implementation Spec' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Use this template to clearly define the customer''s problem, our approach to solving it, and how our solution stands out from competitors. DO NOT make this too long Return in the format", "sections": [{"name": "Customer Identification", "guidelines": "Identify the specific customer or segment experiencing the problem.\n\n### Be Specific\n- Not ''businesses'' but ''B2B SaaS companies with 10-50 employees''\n- Not ''developers'' but ''frontend engineers building React apps''\n- Include their role, company stage, and what they care about most\n\n### Validation\n- How do you know these are your customers?\n- Have you talked to them? How many?\n- What evidence supports this customer choice?"}, {"name": "Problem Definition", "guidelines": "Describe the problem the customer faces in specific, measurable terms.\n\n### Guidelines\n- State the problem from the customer''s perspective, not yours\n- Quantify the pain: time wasted, money lost, frequency of occurrence\n- Distinguish between the **root problem** and symptoms\n\n### Format\n[Customers] currently struggle with [specific problem], which causes [measurable consequence]. Today they cope by [current workaround], which is inadequate because [limitation]."}, {"name": "Solution Approach", "guidelines": "How you will solve the problem — the ''what'' at a high level.\n\n### Guidelines\n- Describe the approach, not every feature\n- Explain why THIS approach vs. alternatives you considered\n- Be honest about what is novel vs. what is table stakes\n\n### Format\nWe will [approach] by [method], which solves the problem because [reasoning]. We chose this over [alternatives] because [tradeoff rationale]."}, {"name": "Competitive Analysis", "guidelines": "Who else solves this problem and why customers would choose you.\n\n### Competitors\nFor each competitor or alternative (including ''do nothing''):\n- **Name**: What they offer\n- **Strengths**: What they do well\n- **Weaknesses**: Where they fall short\n- **Why customers switch**: What triggers leaving them\n\n### Differentiation\nThe specific reasons a customer would choose your solution over every alternative listed above."}, {"name": "Differentiation Strategy", "guidelines": "What makes your solution uniquely valuable — your unfair advantage.\n\n### Types of Differentiation\n- **Experience**: 10x better UX for the specific use case\n- **Speed**: Dramatically faster to start or complete the task\n- **Price**: Significantly cheaper or different pricing model\n- **Integration**: Works where your customers already are\n- **Technology**: Unique capability competitors can''t easily replicate\n\n### The Hypothesis\nComplete the sentence:\n\n**If we help** [customer] **solve** [problem] **with** [approach], **they will choose it over** [competitors] **because our solution is** [differentiation]."}]}' WHERE name = 'Founding Hypothesis' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Focus on actionable, time-bound activities with clear owners and success criteria. Pricing strategy should include competitive context and justification. Promotional plans should specify channels with expected outcomes. The overall plan should be executable by the team, not aspirational marketing fluff.", "sections": [{"name": "Executive Summary", "guidelines": "The entire GTM strategy in one paragraph. A busy executive should be able to read this and know the plan.\n\n### Include\n- **What** you are launching\n- **Who** you are targeting\n- **When** you are launching\n- **How** you will reach them\n- **Goal** for the first 90 days (one key metric)"}, {"name": "Market Analysis", "guidelines": "The landscape you are launching into.\n\n### Market Size\n- **TAM**: Total addressable market\n- **SAM**: Serviceable addressable market\n- **SOM**: Serviceable obtainable market (realistic near-term target)\n\n### Target Segments\nFor each segment:\n- **Who they are**: Demographics, company size, role\n- **Why they buy**: Primary motivation and trigger event\n- **Where they are**: Channels, communities, publications they trust\n- **Current solution**: What they use today and why it''s insufficient\n\n### Competitive Landscape\nKey competitors and your differentiation against each."}, {"name": "Product Positioning", "guidelines": "How the product will be perceived in the market.\n\n### Positioning Statement\nFor [target customer] who [need/pain point], [product name] is a [category] that [key benefit]. Unlike [alternatives], it [key differentiator].\n\n### Messaging Hierarchy\n- **Primary message**: The one thing everyone should remember\n- **Supporting messages**: 3 proof points that back up the primary message\n- **Proof points**: Data, testimonials, or demos that make it believable\n\n### Tone & Voice\nHow the product should feel in all communications: technical, approachable, premium, playful, etc."}, {"name": "Pricing Strategy", "guidelines": "How the product is priced and why.\n\n### Pricing Model\n- **Model type**: Freemium, free trial, subscription, usage-based, one-time, etc.\n- **Tiers**: What each tier includes and who it''s for\n- **Price points**: Specific numbers with justification\n\n### Competitive Context\n- How your pricing compares to alternatives\n- Price anchoring strategy (positioned as premium, value, or disruptive)\n\n### Metrics\n- **Target conversion rate**: Free → paid\n- **Target ARPU**: Average revenue per user\n- **Expansion path**: How users upgrade over time"}, {"name": "Promotional Plan", "guidelines": "How you will generate awareness and demand.\n\n### Launch Channels\nFor each channel:\n- **Channel**: e.g., Product Hunt, Twitter/X, email, content marketing, paid ads\n- **Tactic**: Specific action (e.g., ''Launch on PH with 50 pre-committed upvoters'')\n- **Timeline**: When this happens relative to launch\n- **Owner**: Who is responsible\n- **Budget**: Estimated spend (if any)\n\n### Content Plan\n- Launch blog post / announcement\n- Demo video or product walkthrough\n- Customer stories or case studies\n- Social media content calendar\n\n### PR & Partnerships\n- Press outreach targets\n- Partnership or co-marketing opportunities"}, {"name": "Sales Strategy", "guidelines": "How the product will be sold.\n\n### Sales Motion\n- **Self-serve**: Users sign up and convert on their own\n- **Sales-assisted**: Inbound leads get a demo or call\n- **Outbound**: Proactive outreach to target accounts\n- **Hybrid**: Combination (describe the trigger for each)\n\n### Sales Enablement\n- One-pager or pitch deck\n- Demo script and environment\n- Objection handling guide\n- Competitive battlecard\n\n### Partner & Channel Sales\nAny indirect sales channels: resellers, integrations marketplace, agency partners."}, {"name": "Success Metrics", "guidelines": "How you will know the launch is working.\n\n### 30-Day Targets\n- **Awareness**: Website visits, signups, waitlist additions\n- **Activation**: Users who complete onboarding or key action\n- **Revenue**: MRR, deals closed, or conversion rate\n\n### 90-Day Targets\n- **Retention**: Day-30 and Day-60 retention rates\n- **Growth**: Month-over-month growth rate\n- **Satisfaction**: NPS, CSAT, or qualitative feedback themes\n\n### Tracking\n- Which tools are used (analytics, CRM, support)\n- Who reviews metrics and how often\n- What triggers a pivot or escalation if targets are missed"}]}' WHERE name = 'Go-to-Market Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a creative brief that could be handed directly to a designer or AI page builder. Be specific about copy — write actual headlines, not placeholders. Value propositions should be benefit-focused, not feature-focused. Social proof should feel authentic. Design direction should reference specific visual styles, color palettes, or reference sites rather than vague adjectives like ''modern'' or ''clean.'' The page should have a clear conversion narrative from top to bottom.", "sections": [{"name": "Page Goal & Audience", "guidelines": "### Conversion Goal\nThe single primary action you want visitors to take: sign up, buy, book a demo, join waitlist, download, etc.\n\n### Target Visitor\n- **Traffic source**: Where they come from (paid ad, organic search, Product Hunt, referral, social)\n- **Awareness level**: What they already know about you\n- **Key question**: The #1 thing on their mind when they land\n- **Primary objection**: What might stop them from converting\n\nThis profile shapes every section below — the page should feel like a direct answer to this visitor."}, {"name": "Hero Section", "guidelines": "The first thing visitors see — it must answer three questions instantly: What is this? Who is it for? Why should I care?\n\n### Copy\n- **Headline**: 8 words or fewer, benefit-driven (not clever — clear)\n- **Subheadline**: 1-2 sentences expanding on the value proposition\n- **CTA button**: Exact button text (e.g., ''Start free trial'' not ''Submit'')\n\n### Visual\n- **Hero image/visual**: Product screenshot, demo video, illustration, or animation\n- **Layout**: Full-width, split (text left / visual right), centered, etc.\n- **Above the fold**: Everything visible before scrolling"}, {"name": "Value Propositions", "guidelines": "3-4 key benefits written from the user''s perspective — what they GET, not what the product HAS.\n\n### Format for Each\n- **Headline**: 5-8 words, benefit-focused\n- **Description**: 1-2 sentences explaining how the product delivers this benefit\n- **Visual**: Icon suggestion, illustration, or screenshot\n\n### Example\n- **Ship 10x faster** — AI handles the boilerplate so you focus on what makes your product unique. No more copy-pasting patterns between projects.\n\nOrder from most compelling to least. Address the visitor''s top concerns from the audience section above."}, {"name": "Social Proof", "guidelines": "What builds trust and credibility on this page. Be specific — vague praise doesn''t convert.\n\n### Options (pick 2-4)\n- **Customer testimonials**: Real quotes with name, title, company, and photo\n- **Logo bar**: Companies/brands that use the product\n- **Usage stats**: ''10,000+ teams,'' ''2M documents created,'' etc.\n- **Press mentions**: ''Featured in TechCrunch, Product Hunt #1 of the day''\n- **Case study snippet**: ''Company X increased conversion by 40%''\n- **Awards/badges**: G2, Product Hunt, industry awards\n\nOrder by persuasiveness. Lead with whatever is strongest."}, {"name": "Feature Showcase", "guidelines": "3-5 key features that demonstrate HOW the product delivers the value props above.\n\n### Format for Each\n- **Headline**: Benefit-oriented, not technical (''See changes in real time'' not ''WebSocket integration'')\n- **Description**: 2-3 sentences on how it works and why it matters\n- **Visual**: Screenshot, GIF, short video, or illustration\n- **Layout**: Alternating left/right, tabbed showcase, or interactive demo\n\nOrder from most impressive to least. Each feature should build confidence that the product actually works."}, {"name": "CTA & Conversion", "guidelines": "### Primary CTA\n- **Button copy**: Exact text (action-oriented: ''Start building free'' not ''Get started'')\n- **Form fields**: Only what is absolutely required (email? name + email? just a button?)\n- **Placement**: After hero, after features, sticky footer, etc.\n\n### Secondary CTA\n- **For hesitant visitors**: ''Watch demo,'' ''See pricing,'' ''Talk to sales''\n\n### Trust Builders\n- Risk reducers: ''No credit card required,'' ''14-day free trial,'' ''Cancel anytime''\n- Pricing preview: Show pricing or ''Free for up to X users''\n- Urgency (if authentic): ''Early access pricing,'' ''Limited beta spots''"}, {"name": "Design Direction", "guidelines": "### Visual Style\n- **Color palette**: Primary, secondary, accent (hex codes or descriptive: ''dark navy + electric blue accents'')\n- **Typography**: Sans-serif modern, serif editorial, monospace tech, etc.\n- **Spacing**: Dense/information-rich vs. spacious/premium\n- **Vibe**: Minimal, playful, corporate, developer-focused, luxury, etc.\n\n### References\nList 1-3 existing sites whose visual style to emulate and what specifically to borrow from each.\n\n### Brand Assets\n- Logo, brand fonts, brand colors that must be used\n- Existing design system or component library\n\n### Responsive\n- Mobile-first or desktop-first\n- Key mobile adaptations (collapsed nav, stacked layout, etc.)\n- Animation and interaction preferences (subtle or expressive)"}]}' WHERE name = 'Landing Page Brief' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Keep this genuinely short — it''s a 1-pager, not a comprehensive PRD. Be direct and opinionated rather than exhaustive. The problem should be a single testable hypothesis. Evidence should be 3-5 strong data points. The solution should describe what users experience, not internal architecture. Every section earns its place by being essential to the decision.", "sections": [{"name": "Description", "guidelines": "What is the product? A brief description so readers can quickly grasp what this project is about.\n\n### Guidelines\n- 2-3 sentences maximum\n- A stranger should understand the product after reading this\n- Avoid jargon and internal codenames\n- Answer: What does it do? Who is it for?"}, {"name": "Problem", "guidelines": "The problem as a testable hypothesis. You''ll add evidence in the next section.\n\n### Strong Problem Statement Attributes\n- **Short**: Aim for a single sentence\n- **Focused**: A single clear problem, owned by one team, solvable in a reasonable time\n- **Need-based**: References an unmet need (Jobs-To-Be-Done is useful here)\n- **Has a what AND a why**: What''s going wrong, and why it matters\n- **Solution-agnostic**: Resist jumping to a solution this early\n\n### Examples\n- *Good*: ''Lyft drivers are cancelling rides too often because the passengers are too far away.''\n- *Good*: ''Users are dropping off at too high a rate at the final step of the signup flow.''\n- *Bad*: ''User growth is slowing.'' (too broad, not user-centric)\n- *Bad*: ''Build a loyalty program.'' (assumes a solution)\n- *Bad*: ''Users are bouncing from the signup flow.'' (not focused enough, missing the ''why'')"}, {"name": "Why", "guidelines": "How do we know this is a real problem worth solving? Back up your hypothesis.\n\n### Evidence\nCollect 3-5 strong data points. Quality over quantity:\n- **Quantitative**: Metrics, funnel data, churn analysis, survey results\n- **Qualitative**: User interviews, support tickets, session recordings\n- **Market signals**: Competitor moves, industry trends, analyst reports\n\n### Devil''s Advocate\nTry to convince yourself this ISN''T a real or big enough problem:\n- What gaps exist in your evidence?\n- Is the evidence truly telling you what you think?\n- Could there be a simpler explanation?\n\nYour case doesn''t have to be airtight — but acknowledge the gaps."}, {"name": "Success", "guidelines": "How do we know if we''ve solved this problem?\n\n### Ideal Success Criteria\n- **Specific metric**: [Metric name]\n- **Defined goal**: [X]% increase/decrease\n- **Measurable**: Can be tracked with current tooling\n- **Connected to KPIs**: Ties to your team''s goals\n- **Based on data**: Informed by opportunity size and past experiments\n\n### Guidelines\n- Try hard to make it a concrete number: ''10% increase in X,'' ''50% decrease in Y''\n- Pick a goal that''s believable but ambitious — one your team and leaders would be excited about\n- If a metric truly doesn''t make sense, describe what the world looks like if this is a big success"}, {"name": "Audience", "guidelines": "Who are we building for? It should rarely be ''all users.''\n\n### Specifics\n- **User segment**: New vs. returning? Casual vs. power? Free vs. paid?\n- **Platform**: Mobile, web, both?\n- **Geography**: All markets or specific regions?\n- **Size of segment**: What % of your user base does this represent?\n\n### Why This Segment\nWhy focus here first? Biggest pain, largest opportunity, most strategic, etc."}, {"name": "What", "guidelines": "Roughly, what does the solution look like in the product?\n\n### Guidelines\n- Describe what users will experience, not internal architecture\n- Be as detailed as your team needs (depends on how much is already known)\n- Include rough mockups, wireframes, or references if available\n- Highlight the key interaction moments and any major UX decisions\n\n### Format\nWalk through the core experience:\n1. User starts at [context]\n2. They see/do [interaction]\n3. The result is [outcome]"}, {"name": "How", "guidelines": "How will we build or test this?\n\n### Approach\n- **Build**: Full implementation, ship to all users\n- **Experiment**: A/B test with defined success criteria\n- **Prototype**: Quick test to validate before full build\n- **Manual first**: Do it manually to learn before automating\n\n### Technical Considerations\n- Major technical decisions or constraints\n- Dependencies on other teams or systems\n- Known risks or unknowns"}, {"name": "When", "guidelines": "When does it ship and what are the milestones?\n\n### Timeline\n| Milestone | Target Date | Owner |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| Design complete | [Date] | [Name] |\n| Build complete | [Date] | [Name] |\n| Internal testing | [Date] | [Name] |\n| Ship / Experiment start | [Date] | [Name] |\n| Results review | [Date] | [Name] |\n\n### Confidence Level\nHow confident are you in this timeline? What could slip and why?"}]}' WHERE name = 'Lenny''s 1-Pager' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write objectives that are ambitious but achievable within the stated timeframe. Key results must be specific and measurable with concrete numbers — avoid vague outcomes like \"improve\" or \"increase.\" Each initiative should clearly connect to its parent key result. Use the user''s actual context for timelines, not generic placeholders. Risks should be realistic and paired with actionable mitigations.", "sections": [{"name": "Objective", "guidelines": "Clearly state the main goal you want to achieve. This should be ambitious but achievable.\n\n### Guidelines\n- **Qualitative, not quantitative** — objectives describe the destination, key results measure progress\n- **Inspiring but grounded** — should motivate the team and feel attainable\n- **Time-bound** — specify the quarter or timeframe\n- **Aligned** — should connect to company-level strategy\n\n### Example\n''Become the go-to solution for small teams managing customer feedback'' (Q2 2026)"}, {"name": "Key Results", "guidelines": "Measurable outcomes that indicate progress toward the objective. Aim for 3-5 per objective.\n\n### Format\nKR[#]: [Verb] [metric] from [baseline] to [target] by [date]\n\n### Guidelines\n- Each KR must be **specific and measurable** with a concrete number\n- Include the **current baseline** — you can''t measure progress without a starting point\n- Mix **leading indicators** (actions) and **lagging indicators** (outcomes)\n- Set a **stretch target** (70% achievement = good) not a sandbagged one\n\n### Example\n- KR1: Increase weekly active users from 2,000 to 5,000\n- KR2: Achieve NPS score of 50+ (currently 32)\n- KR3: Reduce average onboarding time from 15 min to 5 min"}, {"name": "Initiatives", "guidelines": "High-level projects or workstreams that will drive the key results. 1-3 initiatives per key result.\n\n### Format\nFor each initiative:\n- **Initiative**: What you will do\n- **Drives KR**: Which key result(s) this supports\n- **Owner**: Who is responsible\n- **Effort estimate**: T-shirt size (S/M/L/XL)\n\n### Guidelines\n- Initiatives are the ''how'' — they connect the measurable KRs to actual work\n- Each should be concrete enough to staff and schedule\n- If an initiative doesn''t clearly drive a KR, question whether it belongs"}, {"name": "Alignment", "guidelines": "How these OKRs connect to the bigger picture.\n\n### Company Alignment\n- Which company-level objective(s) these support\n- How success here contributes to overall business goals\n\n### Cross-Team Dependencies\n- Other teams whose OKRs overlap or depend on yours\n- Shared key results or handoff points\n- Potential conflicts to resolve early\n\n### Strategic Context\nWhy these objectives matter NOW — what market conditions, customer feedback, or business priorities make this the right focus."}, {"name": "Key Stakeholders", "guidelines": "Who is responsible for each key result and initiative.\n\n### Format\n| Role | Person | Responsibility |\n|------|--------|----------------|\n| OKR Owner | [Name] | Overall accountability for the objective |\n| KR1 Lead | [Name] | Drives KR1, reports progress weekly |\n| KR2 Lead | [Name] | Drives KR2, reports progress weekly |\n\n### Escalation Path\nWho makes the call when trade-offs are needed between competing KRs or when a KR is off-track."}, {"name": "Timeline & Checkpoints", "guidelines": "When results are expected and how progress is tracked.\n\n### Milestones\n- **Week 2**: [Early signal or setup milestone]\n- **Month 1**: [First meaningful progress checkpoint]\n- **Mid-quarter**: [Halfway check — are we on track?]\n- **End of quarter**: [Final measurement and scoring]\n\n### Cadence\n- **Weekly**: Quick status update (on track / at risk / off track)\n- **Bi-weekly or monthly**: Deeper review with stakeholders\n- **End of quarter**: Full scoring and retrospective"}, {"name": "Risks and Mitigations", "guidelines": "What could prevent you from hitting these OKRs.\n\n### Format\nFor each risk:\n- **Risk**: What could go wrong\n- **Likelihood**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Impact**: Which KR(s) would be affected\n- **Mitigation**: What you''ll do to prevent or reduce this risk\n- **Contingency**: What you''ll do if it happens anyway\n\n### Common OKR Risks\n- Dependency on another team that has different priorities\n- Metric measurement is harder than expected (no baseline, tooling gaps)\n- Scope creep on initiatives — doing too much, achieving nothing\n- External factors (market shift, competitor launch, regulation)"}, {"name": "Review & Scoring", "guidelines": "How progress will be measured and what happens after the cycle.\n\n### Scoring Method\n- **0.0 – 0.3**: No meaningful progress\n- **0.4 – 0.6**: Some progress but fell short\n- **0.7 – 0.8**: Strong result (target for stretch goals)\n- **0.9 – 1.0**: Fully achieved (goal may have been too easy)\n\n### Review Process\n- When is the end-of-cycle review?\n- What format (written self-assessment, team meeting, leadership review)?\n- How do learnings feed into next cycle''s OKRs?\n\n### Retrospective Questions\n- What drove our best results?\n- What would we do differently?\n- Which OKRs should carry over, evolve, or be dropped?"}]}' WHERE name = 'OKRs' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write in the voice of a press release announcing a successfully launched product. The tone should be confident and newsworthy — written for a major publication, not an internal memo. Quotes should feel authentic and specific to the product. FAQs should address real concerns and tough questions, not softballs. Internal FAQs should surface genuine strategic, operational, and financial considerations.", "sections": [{"name": "Heading", "guidelines": "Name the product in a way the target customer will immediately understand.\n\n### Format\n- **Product name**: Clear, memorable, customer-facing name\n- **Tagline**: One sentence underneath that captures the core value proposition\n\nThe heading should work as a real headline — if a journalist saw it, they''d immediately grasp what''s being announced."}, {"name": "Subheading", "guidelines": "One sentence that describes who the customer is and what benefit they gain.\n\n### Format\n[Product name] helps [target customer] [achieve specific benefit].\n\nThis should be benefit-focused (what the customer gets), not feature-focused (what the product does)."}, {"name": "Summary Paragraph", "guidelines": "The press release lead paragraph — sets the scene for the announcement.\n\n### Format\n**[City], [Media Outlet] — [Proposed Launch Date]** — [Company name] today announced [product name], a [brief description] that [key benefit for customers].\n\n### Guidelines\n- Write as if this is a real wire service press release\n- Lead with the most newsworthy aspect\n- Keep to 3-4 sentences maximum\n- Include a concrete detail that makes it tangible"}, {"name": "Problem Paragraph", "guidelines": "Describe the customer problem this product solves — written entirely from the customer''s perspective.\n\n### Guidelines\n- Start with the customer, not the company: ''Today, [customers] struggle with...''\n- Use specific, vivid details about the pain (time wasted, money lost, frustration)\n- Quantify the problem where possible (''spend 4 hours per week manually...''\n- Make the reader feel the problem before they hear the solution\n- Do NOT mention your product in this paragraph"}, {"name": "Solution Paragraph(s)", "guidelines": "Describe how the product solves the problem — clearly and simply.\n\n### Guidelines\n- Lead with the outcome: ''With [product], [customers] can now [benefit]''\n- Explain how it works in plain language — no jargon\n- Highlight 2-3 key capabilities that directly address the problems above\n- For complex products, use multiple short paragraphs rather than one long one\n- Include a specific example or scenario that makes the solution concrete"}, {"name": "Quotes & Getting Started", "guidelines": "### Company Spokesperson Quote\nA quote from a leader at your company. It should:\n- Express the vision and why this matters\n- Sound like a real person talking, not marketing copy\n- Add context the press release paragraphs didn''t cover\n\n### Customer Quote\nA hypothetical quote from a target customer. It should:\n- Describe the specific benefit they experienced\n- Reference a real use case or workflow\n- Sound authentic and enthusiastic without being over-the-top\n\n### Getting Started\nOne paragraph on how easy it is to start using the product. Include a call-to-action and link."}, {"name": "External FAQs", "guidelines": "Questions a customer, journalist, or analyst would ask after reading the press release.\n\n### Format\n**Q: [Question]**\nA: [Answer]\n\n### Example Questions\n- How much does it cost? / What''s the pricing model?\n- How is this different from [competitor]?\n- When is it available? / How do I get access?\n- What data do you collect and how is it secured?\n- Does it integrate with [common tool]?\n\nAnswers should be direct and specific — no deflecting. If something is TBD, say so."}, {"name": "Internal FAQs", "guidelines": "Questions leadership, the board, or other teams would ask internally.\n\n### Format\n**Q: [Question]**\nA: [Answer]\n\n### Example Questions\n- Why is this the right problem to solve now?\n- What''s the estimated revenue impact or market size?\n- How much will this cost to build and maintain?\n- What are the biggest risks? What could go wrong?\n- How does this fit into our broader product strategy?\n- What do we need from other teams to make this successful?\n\nBe honest and thorough — internal FAQs should surface the hard questions."}]}' WHERE name = 'PR FAQ' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Prioritize clarity and focus — this template works best when concise. The problem section needs concrete evidence (data, user quotes, or research). Write requirements as user stories with clear priority levels (P0 vs P1). The launch plan should specify experiment design with measurable success criteria and a ramp plan.", "sections": [{"name": "Team", "guidelines": "List the people involved and their roles.\n\n### Format\n| Role | Name |\n|------|------|\n| PM | [Name] |\n| Design | [Name] |\n| Engineering Lead | [Name] |\n| Analytics | [Name] |\n| Marketing | [Name] |\n\nInclude anyone with decision-making authority or a key deliverable."}, {"name": "Problem", "guidelines": "The customer problem you''re solving — with evidence that it''s real.\n\n### Format\n**Who**: [Specific customer segment]\n**Problem**: [What they struggle with]\n**Evidence**: [How you know — data, research, support tickets]\n\n### Example (Pinterest topic selector)\n*New users want to see topics they''re interested in when they first sign up. Today, all new users see the same feed of popular content dominated by a few categories like women''s fashion and decor that don''t match the diverse interests of all new users.*\n\n### Guidelines\n- Be specific about who the customer is\n- Link to customer research and data\n- The problem should be clear enough that anyone on the team can explain it"}, {"name": "Goals", "guidelines": "One output goal and 2-3 input metrics that drive it.\n\n### Output Goal\nThe single metric that defines success:\n- **Metric**: [Name]\n- **Target**: [X]% change from [baseline]\n- **Timeframe**: [Period]\n\n### Input Metrics\nLeading indicators that drive the output:\n- Input 1: [Metric and target]\n- Input 2: [Metric and target]\n- Input 3: [Metric and target]\n\n### Non-Goals (Optional)\nThings explicitly out of scope and why.\n\n### Example (Pinterest)\n- **Output**: Grow activation rate by 20% (% of signups who return within 7 days)\n- **Inputs**: # users who visit topic selector, # who select a topic, # of topics selected, # who pin during first session"}, {"name": "Requirements", "guidelines": "What the solution looks like, written as user stories with clear priorities.\n\n### High-Level Solution\nDescribe the solution in 2-3 sentences before diving into details.\n\n### Milestones\nIf there are multiple phases, list them clearly with scope for each.\n\n### User Stories\nFor each milestone, write features as user stories:\n- **P0** (must ship): As a [user], I want [capability] so that [benefit]\n- **P0**: As a [user], I want [capability] so that [benefit]\n- **P1** (should ship): As a [user], I want [capability] so that [benefit]\n\n### Open Questions\nHighlight any decisions that need async discussion. Tag the person who should weigh in."}, {"name": "Launch Plan", "guidelines": "How the feature gets to users — staged rollout or experiment.\n\n### Experiment Design (if applicable)\n- **Success criteria**: [Metric] improves by [X]%\n- **Eligibility**: Who is included in the experiment\n- **Test group**: [Description of the new experience]\n- **Control group**: [Description of the current experience]\n- **Split**: [Percentage split, e.g., 50/50]\n- **Duration**: [How long to run for statistical significance]\n\n### Rollout Plan\n- **Phase 1**: [% of users] — Monitor for [duration]\n- **Phase 2**: [% of users] — Monitor for [duration]\n- **Phase 3**: 100% — GA launch\n\n### Rollback Criteria\nWhat metric movement would trigger a rollback? Who makes that call?"}]}' WHERE name = 'Peter''s PRD' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a practical, actionable brief that can be handed directly to an AI coding assistant. Be specific and concrete — avoid vague language. Name specific frameworks, libraries, and patterns in technical sections. Keep it scannable with bullets and short paragraphs. Focus on what the AI needs to know to build this correctly: clear requirements, explicit edge cases, and concrete technical choices. The brief should be self-contained — an engineer or AI agent reading only this document should be able to build the product.", "sections": [{"name": "tl;dr", "guidelines": "A short 2-3 sentence summary explaining the problem being solved, core benefits, key features, and target audience. This should be specific enough that someone could explain the product after reading only this section."}, {"name": "Goals", "guidelines": "### Business Goals\nList 3-5 measurable objectives aligned with the company strategy. Each goal should have a clear metric.\n\n### User Goals\nList 3-5 key benefits or outcomes for the end user. Focus on how the product solves a real user need.\n\n### Non-Goals\nIdentify 2-3 items explicitly out of scope to keep the project focused and prevent scope creep."}, {"name": "User Stories", "guidelines": "List user personas and their stories. For each persona, include 1-5 user stories in the format: As a [USER TYPE], I want to [ACTION], so that [BENEFIT]. Stories should cover the core happy path as well as important secondary flows. Group stories by persona."}, {"name": "Functional Requirements", "guidelines": "List features and functionality grouped by product area and priority:\n- Feature Group (Priority: P0/P1/P2)\n- Feature Name: Description of what it does and any key constraints\n\nP0 = must ship, P1 = should ship, P2 = nice to have. Be specific about behavior, not just naming features."}, {"name": "User Experience", "guidelines": "Describe the end-to-end UX flow that the AI should implement.\n\n**Entry Point & First-Time Experience**\nHow users discover or access the product. Any onboarding steps or empty states.\n\n**Core Experience**\nStep-by-step UI flows with specific component choices:\n- Step 1: What the user does first. UI elements, validation, navigation.\n- Step 2: Next action or screen. Continue for each major step.\n\n**Edge Cases**\nError states, empty states, loading states, and uncommon scenarios to handle."}, {"name": "Narrative", "guidelines": "A short (200-300 word) user-centric story that walks through a realistic scenario: introduce the user and their challenge, show how the product addresses it, and conclude with the positive outcome. This helps the AI understand the intended feel of the product."}, {"name": "Success Metrics", "guidelines": "### User Metrics\nAdoption, satisfaction, and usage metrics (e.g., DAU, task completion rate, NPS).\n\n### Business Metrics\nRevenue, conversion, or cost savings targets.\n\n### Technical Metrics\nPerformance, uptime, or error rate goals.\n\n### Key Events to Track\nSpecific user events to instrument for analytics (e.g., button clicks, form submissions, feature usage)."}, {"name": "Technical Considerations", "guidelines": "High-level technical factors that influence architecture decisions. Describe constraints, performance requirements, and integration needs. Call out any non-obvious technical decisions the AI should be aware of."}, {"name": "UI Architecture", "guidelines": "Describe the frontend architecture:\n- **Framework**: React, Next.js, Svelte, Vue, etc.\n- **Component Library**: shadcn/ui, Material UI, Chakra UI, Bootstrap, or custom\n- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS, CSS Modules, styled-components, etc.\n- **State Management**: React Query, Zustand, Redux, or built-in\n- **Animations**: Framer Motion, CSS transitions, etc.\n\nSpecify the approach for responsive design and accessibility."}, {"name": "API & Backend", "guidelines": "Describe the backend architecture:\n- **Framework**: Next.js API routes, Express, FastAPI, etc.\n- **Database**: Postgres, MySQL, MongoDB, Supabase, etc. with ORM choice (Prisma, Drizzle, etc.)\n- **Authentication**: Clerk, NextAuth, Supabase Auth, etc.\n- **Hosting**: Vercel, AWS, Railway, Fly.io, etc.\n- **Key API endpoints**: List the main endpoints needed with their purpose."}, {"name": "Performance & Scalability", "guidelines": "Describe performance targets and scalability approach:\n- **Optimizations**: Lazy loading, caching strategy, SSR/SSG, image optimization\n- **Accessibility**: WCAG compliance level and key requirements\n- **Scalability**: Expected user load, database scaling approach, CDN strategy\n- **Monitoring**: Error tracking, performance monitoring, logging approach"}, {"name": "Integration Points", "guidelines": "List all third-party services, APIs, and integrations the product needs. For each, specify: what it does, which SDK/library to use, and any configuration needed. Include payment processors, email services, analytics, AI/ML APIs, and any other external dependencies."}]}' WHERE name IN ('Product Brief for AI', 'Product Brief for AI Development') AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Be exhaustive and specific. Each checklist item should be a concrete, verifiable task with a clear definition of done — not a vague category. Organize items chronologically (T-4 weeks, T-2 weeks, T-1 week, launch day, post-launch). Include cross-functional dependencies between teams.", "sections": [{"name": "Pre-Launch (T-4 weeks)", "guidelines": "Tasks to complete well before launch day.\n\n### Product Readiness\n- [ ] All P0 features complete and tested\n- [ ] Beta feedback addressed or triaged\n- [ ] Performance and load testing passed\n- [ ] Security review completed\n- [ ] Accessibility audit passed\n\n### Content & Marketing\n- [ ] Launch blog post drafted and reviewed\n- [ ] Product screenshots and demo video created\n- [ ] Social media posts scheduled\n- [ ] Email announcement drafted\n- [ ] Press/media outreach started (if applicable)\n\n### Internal Readiness\n- [ ] Support team trained on new features\n- [ ] FAQ and help docs written\n- [ ] Internal announcement sent to company"}, {"name": "Pre-Launch (T-1 week)", "guidelines": "Final preparations the week before launch.\n\n### Technical\n- [ ] Staging environment matches production config\n- [ ] Monitoring and alerting configured\n- [ ] Rollback plan documented and tested\n- [ ] Feature flags set to correct state\n- [ ] Database migrations tested in staging\n\n### Marketing\n- [ ] Launch page live (or scheduled)\n- [ ] All marketing materials finalized and approved\n- [ ] Social media queue loaded\n- [ ] Email list segmented and ready\n- [ ] Product Hunt / Hacker News draft prepared (if applicable)\n\n### Team\n- [ ] Launch day schedule shared with team\n- [ ] On-call rotation set for launch day\n- [ ] Escalation contacts identified"}, {"name": "Launch Day", "guidelines": "The day-of execution checklist.\n\n### Morning\n- [ ] Deploy to production\n- [ ] Verify all features working in production\n- [ ] Enable feature flags / remove beta restrictions\n- [ ] Publish launch blog post\n- [ ] Send email announcement\n\n### Midday\n- [ ] Post on social media channels\n- [ ] Submit to Product Hunt / communities\n- [ ] Monitor error rates and performance dashboards\n- [ ] Respond to early user feedback and questions\n\n### End of Day\n- [ ] Review launch day metrics\n- [ ] Address any critical bugs or issues\n- [ ] Send internal recap to team\n- [ ] Celebrate the launch"}, {"name": "Post-Launch (Week 1)", "guidelines": "First week after launch — monitor, respond, and iterate.\n\n### Monitoring\n- [ ] Track key metrics daily (signups, activation, errors)\n- [ ] Monitor support ticket volume and themes\n- [ ] Watch social media mentions and sentiment\n- [ ] Review error tracking (Sentry, LogRocket) for new issues\n\n### Response\n- [ ] Respond to all customer feedback within 24 hours\n- [ ] Triage and fix critical bugs immediately\n- [ ] Publish follow-up content (tips, tutorials, use cases)\n\n### Retrospective\n- [ ] Run launch retrospective with team\n- [ ] Document learnings for next launch\n- [ ] Update launch playbook with improvements"}, {"name": "Post-Launch (Month 1)", "guidelines": "First month — measure success and plan next steps.\n\n### Metrics Review\n- [ ] Compare actual metrics to launch targets\n- [ ] Identify top-performing acquisition channels\n- [ ] Analyze activation funnel drop-off points\n- [ ] Assess early retention signals\n\n### Product Iteration\n- [ ] Prioritize feature requests from launch feedback\n- [ ] Ship quick wins identified during launch week\n- [ ] Plan next release based on user data\n\n### Growth\n- [ ] Double down on channels that worked\n- [ ] Cut or adjust channels that underperformed\n- [ ] Plan second wave of marketing (case studies, webinars, partnerships)"}, {"name": "Launch Roles & Contacts", "guidelines": "Who is responsible for what during the launch.\n\n### Key Roles\n| Role | Person | Responsibility |\n|------|--------|----------------|\n| Launch Lead | [Name] | Overall coordination, go/no-go decision |\n| Engineering | [Name] | Deploy, monitor, hotfix |\n| Marketing | [Name] | Content, social, PR |\n| Support | [Name] | Customer responses, FAQ updates |\n| Design | [Name] | Assets, last-minute visual fixes |\n\n### Escalation Path\n- **Critical bug**: [Who to contact and how]\n- **PR/comms issue**: [Who to contact]\n- **Go/no-go decision**: [Who makes the call]"}]}' WHERE name = 'Product Launch Checklist' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Be specific to the product''s actual architecture and tech stack — avoid generic security checklists. Prioritize risks by likelihood and impact, not just listing every possible threat. Include concrete remediation steps with estimated effort. The incident response plan should be actionable enough to follow during an actual security event.", "sections": [{"name": "Product Overview", "guidelines": "Foundation for the security assessment — what is being assessed.\n\n### Product Description\n- Core functionality and target users\n- Distinguishing features that create unique security needs\n- Potential attack vectors specific to this product\n\n### Technical Architecture\n- Programming languages, frameworks, and key libraries\n- Infrastructure: cloud provider, hosting model (containers, serverless, VMs)\n- Network architecture: public endpoints, internal services, VPNs\n\n### Dependencies\n- Third-party services, APIs, and libraries\n- Known vulnerabilities in dependencies (check npm audit, Dependabot, etc.)\n- SLA and security posture of critical vendors\n\n### Deployment Environment\n- On-premises / Cloud / Hybrid\n- Environment isolation (prod, staging, dev)\n- Secrets management approach"}, {"name": "Data Security", "guidelines": "How sensitive data is protected at rest, in transit, and in use.\n\n### Data Classification\n- **Critical**: Passwords, tokens, payment info, API keys\n- **Sensitive**: PII (email, name, phone), health data, financial records\n- **Internal**: Business logic, analytics, logs\n- **Public**: Marketing content, public API docs\n\n### Protection Measures\n- **Encryption at rest**: Database encryption, column-level encryption\n- **Encryption in transit**: TLS version and configuration\n- **Hashing**: Password hashing algorithm (bcrypt, argon2, scrypt)\n- **Tokenization**: Payment data or other sensitive fields\n- **Data integrity**: Checksums, audit trails, backup verification\n\n### Privacy & Compliance\n- GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, SOC 2 applicability\n- Data retention policies and automated deletion\n- Right to deletion / data export process\n- Data processing agreements with vendors"}, {"name": "Authentication & Authorization", "guidelines": "How users prove who they are and what they can access.\n\n### Authentication\n- **Methods**: Password, OAuth, SSO, magic link, MFA\n- **Session management**: Token type (JWT, session cookies), expiration, refresh\n- **Password policy**: Minimum length, complexity, breach checking\n- **Rate limiting**: Login attempts, account lockout policy\n\n### Authorization\n- **Model**: RBAC, ABAC, or custom\n- **Roles**: List each role and its permissions\n- **Enforcement**: Where access control is checked (middleware, API, database RLS)\n- **Principle of least privilege**: How it is applied\n\n### API Security\n- API key management and rotation\n- OAuth scopes and token permissions\n- Rate limiting and throttling per endpoint"}, {"name": "Threat Analysis & Risk Assessment", "guidelines": "Identifying, evaluating, and prioritizing security risks.\n\n### Threat Model\nFor each threat category:\n- **Threat**: [Description]\n- **Attack vector**: [How an attacker would exploit this]\n- **Likelihood**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Impact**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Current controls**: [What is already in place]\n\n### Common Threats to Assess\n- **Injection**: SQL injection, XSS, command injection\n- **Authentication bypass**: Broken auth, session hijacking\n- **Data exposure**: Sensitive data in logs, error messages, APIs\n- **Access control**: Privilege escalation, IDOR\n- **Supply chain**: Compromised dependencies, malicious packages\n- **Infrastructure**: Misconfigured cloud services, exposed ports\n\n### Risk Prioritization\nRank risks by (likelihood × impact) to focus remediation efforts."}, {"name": "Security Controls & Monitoring", "guidelines": "Defensive measures and ongoing security operations.\n\n### Preventive Controls\n- Input validation and sanitization strategy\n- Content Security Policy (CSP) headers\n- CORS configuration\n- Dependency scanning (Snyk, npm audit, Dependabot)\n- Code review security checklist\n\n### Detective Controls\n- **Logging**: What security events are logged and where\n- **Monitoring**: Real-time alerting on suspicious activity\n- **Audit trails**: Who changed what, when, from where\n- **Vulnerability scanning**: Frequency and tools used\n\n### Security Testing\n- Static analysis (SAST): Tools and CI integration\n- Dynamic analysis (DAST): Penetration testing schedule\n- Bug bounty program: If applicable, scope and rewards"}, {"name": "Incident Response & Business Continuity", "guidelines": "What happens when something goes wrong.\n\n### Incident Response Plan\n- **Detection**: How incidents are identified (alerts, reports, monitoring)\n- **Triage**: Severity classification (P0-P3) and response times\n- **Containment**: Immediate steps to limit damage\n- **Communication**: Who is notified, internal and external disclosure\n- **Recovery**: Steps to restore normal operations\n- **Post-mortem**: Root cause analysis and prevention\n\n### Contacts\n| Role | Person | Contact |\n|------|--------|--------|\n| Security lead | [Name] | [Email/phone] |\n| Engineering on-call | [Name] | [Pager] |\n| Legal/compliance | [Name] | [Email] |\n| Communications | [Name] | [Email] |\n\n### Business Continuity\n- **Backup strategy**: Frequency, retention, tested recovery time\n- **Disaster recovery**: RTO and RPO targets\n- **Failover**: Redundancy and auto-scaling configuration\n\n### Remediation Priorities\nSummary of the most critical vulnerabilities found in this assessment with remediation timelines and owners."}]}' WHERE name = 'Product Security Assessment' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Think long-term and connect every section back to the product vision. Market analysis should include specific data and trends, not generic industry observations. OKRs should ladder clearly from vision to measurable outcomes. The roadmap should explain sequencing rationale — why this order matters. Avoid filler; every section should contain decisions or insights that change behavior.", "sections": [{"name": "Product Vision & Mission", "guidelines": "The overarching purpose and long-term aspiration of the product.\n\n### Vision\nThe future state your product is working toward. This should be:\n- Ambitious and inspiring — paints a picture of the world you want to create\n- Aligned with the company''s broader vision\n- Stable over years, not quarters\n\n### Mission\nWhat your product does today to move toward that vision:\n- Who you serve\n- What value you deliver\n- How you are uniquely positioned to deliver it\n\n### Example\n- **Vision**: ''Every team ships great products without bureaucracy.''\n- **Mission**: ''We give product teams AI-powered tools to write better specs, align faster, and ship with confidence.''"}, {"name": "Market Analysis", "guidelines": "The landscape your product operates in.\n\n### Industry Trends\n- 3-5 current and emerging trends that affect your product\n- For each: what the trend is, how it affects your market, and your strategic response\n\n### Competitor Analysis\nFor each key competitor:\n- **Name**: What they do\n- **Strengths**: Where they excel\n- **Weaknesses**: Where they fall short\n- **Your advantage**: Why customers choose you instead\n\n### Target Audience\n- **Primary segment**: Who they are, what they need, how large the segment is\n- **Secondary segment**: Who else benefits from your product\n- **Underserved needs**: Gaps in the market that your product can fill\n\n### Market Size\n- **TAM / SAM / SOM**: With sources and methodology"}, {"name": "Objectives & Key Results", "guidelines": "Precise, measurable goals for the strategy period.\n\n### Objectives\nFor each objective:\n- **Objective**: [Ambitious, time-bound goal aligned with the vision]\n- **Timeframe**: [Quarter or year]\n- **Owner**: [Who is accountable]\n\n### Key Results\nFor each objective, 2-4 measurable results:\n- KR1: [Metric] from [baseline] to [target] by [date]\n- KR2: [Metric] from [baseline] to [target] by [date]\n\n### Alignment\nHow these OKRs connect to:\n- Company-level objectives\n- Cross-functional team goals\n- Revenue and growth targets"}, {"name": "Product Strategies", "guidelines": "Strategic approaches across product, marketing, and customer success.\n\n### Product Development Strategy\n- Key bets: The 2-3 biggest things you are building and why\n- Build vs. buy vs. partner decisions\n- Technical investments (platform, infrastructure, AI/ML)\n- UX/UI evolution direction\n\n### Marketing & Sales Strategy\n- Positioning and messaging approach\n- Key channels and campaigns\n- Sales motion (self-serve, sales-assisted, enterprise)\n- Pricing strategy and evolution\n\n### Customer Success Strategy\n- Onboarding and activation approach\n- Retention and expansion playbook\n- Support model and escalation paths\n- Community and education programs"}, {"name": "Product Roadmap", "guidelines": "A time-bound plan showing what gets built and when.\n\n### Near-Term (This Quarter)\nFor each initiative:\n- **Initiative**: [Name]\n- **Goal**: [What it achieves]\n- **Status**: Not started / In progress / Complete\n- **Dependencies**: [What it needs]\n\n### Medium-Term (Next 2 Quarters)\n- Planned initiatives with rationale and rough sizing\n- Key milestones and checkpoints\n\n### Long-Term (6-12 Months)\n- Strategic bets and exploration areas\n- Technology investments\n- Market expansion plans\n\n### Sequencing Rationale\nWhy this order? What must come first to enable what comes later?"}, {"name": "Risks & Success Criteria", "guidelines": "What could derail the strategy and how you will measure progress.\n\n### Key Risks\nFor each risk:\n- **Risk**: [What could go wrong]\n- **Likelihood**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Impact**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Mitigation**: [How to prevent or reduce]\n\n### Strategy Success Criteria\nHow you will know the strategy is working at the 3, 6, and 12 month mark:\n- **3 months**: [Early signals and leading indicators]\n- **6 months**: [Meaningful progress markers]\n- **12 months**: [Outcome-level results]\n\n### Review Cadence\nWhen and how the strategy will be reviewed and updated."}]}' WHERE name = 'Product Strategy Document' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a pragmatic engineering plan, not an aspirational architecture doc. The current state section should be honest about what is actually wrong — with specific examples of pain points, not vague complaints. The target state should be concrete enough to validate when you''re done. Migration steps should be ordered so each step leaves the codebase in a working state. Timebox the effort — refactors without deadlines never finish. Be explicit about what is NOT being refactored.", "sections": [{"name": "Current State", "guidelines": "What exists today and what is specifically wrong with it.\n\n### Problem Areas\nFor each problem, be concrete:\n- **Area**: [Module/file/pattern]\n- **What''s wrong**: [Specific issue — not ''it''s messy'' but ''3 different auth patterns across 12 files'']\n- **Impact**: [Build time, error rate, developer confusion, incidents caused]\n\n### Quantified Pain\n- Build/deploy time: X minutes\n- Incident frequency: X per month related to this area\n- Developer hours wasted: X hours/week\n- Code duplication: X instances of similar logic"}, {"name": "Target State", "guidelines": "What the code looks like when the refactor is done.\n\n### Architecture Changes\nDescribe the new patterns, abstractions, or structure at a level specific enough to know when you''re done.\n\n### Before → After\n- **Before**: [Current pattern with specific example]\n- **After**: [New pattern with specific example]\n\n### Completion Criteria\nObjectively verifiable — not ''code is cleaner'' but:\n- [ ] All API routes use the new middleware pattern\n- [ ] Zero instances of the deprecated `oldFunction()` remain\n- [ ] All database queries go through the repository layer\n- [ ] Test coverage for refactored modules is above X%"}, {"name": "Motivation & Urgency", "guidelines": "### Why Now\nThe trigger that made this the right time:\n- Blocked feature? Scaling issue? Recurring incidents? Team growing?\n\n### Business Case\n- **Cost of NOT refactoring**: [Quantified: incidents, velocity loss, hiring friction]\n- **Value of refactoring**: [What it unblocks: features, performance, team speed]\n- **Time investment**: [Estimated effort in days/weeks]\n- **Break-even**: [When the investment pays off]\n\n### Stakeholder Alignment\nWho needs to approve this and what they care about (timeline impact, risk, feature delay)."}, {"name": "Scope & Boundaries", "guidelines": "### In Scope\nExplicitly list what IS being changed:\n- Files/modules: [list]\n- Patterns/abstractions: [list]\n- Dependencies: [list]\n\n### Out of Scope\nExplicitly list what is NOT being changed and why:\n- [Module X]: Not touched because [reason]\n- [Pattern Y]: Separate refactor planned for [timeframe]\n\n### Phases\nIf the refactor can be broken into independent phases:\n- **Phase 1**: [Scope] → [Deliverable] → [Can ship independently: yes/no]\n- **Phase 2**: [Scope] → [Deliverable]\n- **Phase 3**: [Scope] → [Deliverable]"}, {"name": "Migration Strategy", "guidelines": "How to get from current → target state while keeping the system working throughout.\n\n### Approach\n- **Strangler fig**: New pattern alongside old, gradually migrate callers\n- **Big bang**: One switchover (higher risk, faster completion)\n- **Incremental**: Gradual replacement file by file\n\n### Coexistence Plan\nIf old and new patterns coexist temporarily:\n- How are they distinguished? (naming convention, directory, feature flag)\n- How long will both exist?\n- What prevents new code from using the old pattern?\n\n### Data Migration\nIf schema or data format changes:\n- Migration script approach\n- Backfill strategy\n- Rollback capability"}, {"name": "Implementation Steps", "guidelines": "Ordered list of concrete steps. Each step should leave the codebase in a working, deployable state.\n\n### Format\n```\nStep 1: [Short description]\nFiles: [list of files changed]\nMigration: [any schema changes]\nVerification: [how to confirm it works]\nPR size: [small/medium/large]\n```\n\n### Step Sequence\n1. [First change — usually the new abstraction/pattern]\n2. [Migrate highest-value or highest-risk area first]\n3. [Continue migration area by area]\n4. [Remove old code and dead paths]\n5. [Update documentation and tests]\n\nFlag any steps that require team coordination or have deploy ordering constraints."}, {"name": "Risk Assessment & Rollback", "guidelines": "### Risk Matrix\nFor each risk:\n- **Risk**: What could go wrong\n- **Likelihood**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Impact**: Low / Medium / High\n- **Mitigation**: How to prevent or detect early\n- **Rollback**: How to revert if it happens\n\n### Common Risks\n- Data loss during migration\n- Broken integrations with external services\n- Performance regression under production load\n- Blocked feature work during refactor\n\n### Monitoring\n- Error rates to watch during/after deploy\n- Performance metrics to compare before/after\n- Alerts to set up before starting\n\n### Point of No Return\nIdentify any steps that cannot be easily reversed (schema migrations, data transformations) and what safeguards to put in place."}]}' WHERE name = 'Refactoring Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Fill in each section with specific details relevant to the release being documented.", "sections": [{"name": "Release Information", "guidelines": "### Metadata\n- **Product**: [Product name]\n- **Version**: [X.Y.Z]\n- **Release date**: [Date]\n- **Release type**: Major / Minor / Patch / Hotfix\n\n### Summary\nOne paragraph summarizing what this release delivers and who it''s for. Lead with the most impactful change."}, {"name": "New Features", "guidelines": "Features and capabilities added in this release.\n\n### Format for Each Feature\n**[Feature Name]**\n[1-2 sentence description of what it does and why it matters to users.]\n\n### Guidelines\n- Lead with the user benefit, not the technical implementation\n- Include a screenshot or GIF if the feature is visual\n- Note if a feature is in beta, behind a feature flag, or limited to certain plans\n- If no new features, state: ''No new features in this release.''"}, {"name": "Improvements", "guidelines": "Enhancements to existing features — performance gains, UX polish, and quality-of-life changes.\n\n### Format\n- **[Area]**: [What improved and why it matters]\n\n### Examples\n- **Dashboard loading**: Reduced load time from 3s to 800ms\n- **Search**: Now supports partial matching and highlights results\n- **Mobile**: Improved touch targets and navigation on small screens\n\nIf no improvements, state: ''No improvements in this release.''"}, {"name": "Bug Fixes", "guidelines": "Bugs resolved in this release.\n\n### Format\n- **[Bug summary]**: [What was fixed and when it was introduced if known]\n\n### Guidelines\n- Write from the user''s perspective: ''Fixed an issue where [user action] would [broken behavior]''\n- Group by area (UI, API, Performance, etc.) if there are many\n- For security fixes, note the severity without disclosing exploit details\n\nIf no bug fixes, state: ''No bug fixes in this release.''"}, {"name": "Known Issues", "guidelines": "Issues the team is aware of but not yet resolved.\n\n### Format\nFor each issue:\n- **Issue**: [Description of the problem]\n- **Impact**: [Who is affected and how]\n- **Workaround**: [Temporary solution if one exists]\n- **Status**: Investigating / In progress / Planned for [version]\n\nIf no known issues, state: ''No known issues.''"}, {"name": "Breaking Changes & Migration", "guidelines": "Changes that require user action or may affect existing workflows.\n\n### Format\n**[What changed]**\n- **Before**: [Previous behavior]\n- **After**: [New behavior]\n- **Action required**: [What users need to do]\n- **Deadline**: [When the old behavior will be removed, if applicable]\n\n### Deprecations\nFeatures or APIs that are deprecated and will be removed in a future release.\n\nIf no breaking changes, state: ''No breaking changes in this release.''"}]}' WHERE name = 'Release Notes' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write with enough specificity that any team member could execute the plan independently. Include concrete dates, owners, and dependencies. Be brutally clear about what is included vs. excluded from scope. Success criteria should be measurable from day one. The RACI matrix should cover every major workstream with no ambiguity.", "sections": [{"name": "Product Overview", "guidelines": "What is being released, who it is for, and why it matters.\n\n### Summary\n- **Product/Feature**: [Name and brief description]\n- **Target audience**: [Who benefits from this release]\n- **Value proposition**: [Key benefit in one sentence]\n- **Strategic alignment**: How this release supports broader product/business goals\n\n### Release Type\n- Major release / Feature launch / Improvement / Hotfix\n- New product vs. update to existing"}, {"name": "Release Objectives", "guidelines": "What this release aims to achieve — using SMART goals.\n\n### Primary Objectives\nFor each objective:\n- **Goal**: [Specific, measurable outcome]\n- **Metric**: [How it will be measured]\n- **Target**: [Concrete number or threshold]\n- **Timeline**: [When we expect to see results]\n\n### Relevance\nHow each objective ties to team KPIs and company strategy.\n\n### Achievability\nAssessment of feasibility given time and resources. Flag any objectives that are stretch goals."}, {"name": "Release Scope", "guidelines": "What is and isn''t included in this release.\n\n### Included Features\nFor each feature:\n- **Feature**: [Name] — [Brief description and purpose]\n- **Priority**: P0 / P1 / P2\n\n### Bug Fixes\n- [Bug description] — [Impact and urgency]\n\n### Excluded / Deferred\n- [Feature/fix] — Deferred because [reason]. Planned for [timeframe].\n\n### Non-Functional Requirements\n- **Performance**: [Targets — load time, throughput, etc.]\n- **Security**: [Requirements — auth, encryption, audit logging]\n- **Accessibility**: [WCAG level and specific requirements]\n\n### Dependencies & Limitations\n- External dependencies that could affect the release\n- Known limitations of the current release"}, {"name": "Stakeholders & RACI", "guidelines": "Who is involved and what they are responsible for.\n\n### RACI Matrix\n| Activity | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n|----------|-------------|-------------|-----------|----------|\n| Feature development | [Name/Team] | [Name] | [Names] | [Names] |\n| QA & testing | [Name/Team] | [Name] | [Names] | [Names] |\n| Go/no-go decision | [Name] | [Name] | [Names] | [Names] |\n| Marketing & comms | [Name/Team] | [Name] | [Names] | [Names] |\n| Customer support | [Name/Team] | [Name] | [Names] | [Names] |\n\n### External Stakeholders\n- Partners, vendors, or customers involved in the release\n- Their roles and communication plan"}, {"name": "Release Timeline & Milestones", "guidelines": "The schedule from now to release and beyond.\n\n### Key Milestones\n| Milestone | Date | Owner | Status |\n|-----------|------|-------|--------|\n| Feature freeze | [Date] | [Name] | |\n| QA complete | [Date] | [Name] | |\n| Staging deploy | [Date] | [Name] | |\n| Go/no-go decision | [Date] | [Name] | |\n| Production deploy | [Date] | [Name] | |\n| Post-release review | [Date] | [Name] | |\n\n### Deploy Plan\n- **Strategy**: Big bang / Rolling / Canary / Feature flag\n- **Rollback trigger**: [What metric or condition triggers rollback]\n- **Rollback procedure**: [Steps to revert]"}, {"name": "Risk Assessment", "guidelines": "What could go wrong and how to handle it.\n\n### Risk Matrix\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |\n|------|------------|--------|------------|-------|\n| [Risk 1] | Low/Med/High | Low/Med/High | [Plan] | [Name] |\n| [Risk 2] | Low/Med/High | Low/Med/High | [Plan] | [Name] |\n\n### Common Release Risks\n- Critical bug found in staging\n- External dependency not ready\n- Performance degradation under production load\n- Negative user feedback requiring rapid response\n\n### Go/No-Go Criteria\nSpecific conditions that must be met for the release to proceed. Who makes the final call?"}, {"name": "Measurement & Success Criteria", "guidelines": "How you will know the release was successful.\n\n### Day-1 Metrics\n- Error rate stays below [threshold]\n- Performance within [targets]\n- No P0 bugs reported\n\n### Week-1 Metrics\n- [Feature adoption metric]: [Target]\n- [User satisfaction metric]: [Target]\n- Support ticket volume: [Expected vs. threshold]\n\n### Month-1 Metrics\n- [Business outcome metric]: [Target]\n- [Retention/engagement metric]: [Target]\n\n### Review\n- When is the post-release review?\n- What format (metrics dashboard, team meeting, written report)?\n- How do learnings feed into the next release cycle?"}]}' WHERE name = 'Release Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write this as a clear, complete spec that someone could paste into v0, Bolt, Lovable, or Replit and get a working prototype on the first try. Be extremely specific about visual design (name exact component libraries, colors, layouts) and user interactions (what happens on every click, submit, and navigation). Tech stack choices should be explicit — these tools need exact framework and library names, not categories. Keep the language direct and imperative. The spec should be self-contained: an AI code generator reading only this document should produce something that looks and works right without follow-up questions.", "sections": [{"name": "Product Overview", "guidelines": "What you are building in 2-3 sentences: the core concept, who it is for, and the primary action users take. Be specific about what makes this product useful — not just what it is, but why someone would use it. Include the scope for this prototype: what is included and what will be built later."}, {"name": "Design & Visual Style", "guidelines": "Specific design direction for the AI tool to follow:\n- **Component library**: shadcn/ui, Material UI, Chakra UI, DaisyUI, or custom\n- **Styling framework**: Tailwind CSS, CSS Modules, vanilla CSS\n- **Color scheme**: Primary, secondary, and accent colors (hex codes or descriptive palette like ''neutral with blue accents'')\n- **Theme**: Light mode, dark mode, or both with toggle\n- **Layout style**: Sidebar nav, top nav, dashboard grid, single-page scroll, etc.\n- **Typography & spacing**: Dense/compact vs. spacious, font preferences\n- **Reference sites**: 1-2 existing products whose visual style to emulate"}, {"name": "Tech Stack", "guidelines": "The exact technologies the prototype should use. Be specific — AI tools generate better code with explicit choices:\n- **Framework**: Next.js (App Router), Vite + React, Astro, SvelteKit, Nuxt, etc.\n- **Language**: TypeScript or JavaScript\n- **Database**: Supabase, Neon, PlanetScale, Firebase, or local SQLite\n- **ORM**: Prisma, Drizzle, or direct SQL\n- **Auth**: Clerk, NextAuth, Supabase Auth, or none for prototype\n- **Hosting target**: Vercel, Netlify, Railway, or local only\n- **Key libraries**: Any specific packages to use (date handling, charts, rich text, etc.)"}, {"name": "Pages & Navigation", "guidelines": "Every page or screen in the prototype with its URL path and purpose:\n- **Page name** (/path): What the user sees and does on this page\n\nSpecify the navigation structure: sidebar links, top nav items, tab bars, or breadcrumbs. Note which pages require authentication and which are public. Describe the landing/home page experience for both logged-in and logged-out users."}, {"name": "Core User Flows", "guidelines": "The 2-4 most important user interactions, described step by step:\n\n**Flow 1: [Name]**\n1. User lands on [page] and sees [what]\n2. User clicks/types [action]\n3. System responds with [result]\n4. User sees [confirmation/next state]\n\nInclude the ''first win'' moment — the first time the user gets value from the product. Cover the happy path in detail. Note key transitions, loading states, and success/error feedback."}, {"name": "Data Model & Backend", "guidelines": "The core data entities the prototype needs, with key fields:\n- **Entity** (field: type, field: type, ...)\n- Relationships between entities\n\nSpecify any API endpoints or server actions needed. Note if the prototype should use real data persistence or mock/seed data. Include any background jobs, webhooks, or scheduled tasks if relevant."}, {"name": "Key Components", "guidelines": "Specific UI components that need special attention beyond standard page layouts. Examples: a drag-and-drop kanban board, a real-time chat widget, a rich text editor, a data table with sorting/filtering, a multi-step form wizard, a file upload area. For each, describe the expected behavior and any library preferences."}, {"name": "AI Generation Notes", "guidelines": "Additional instructions specifically for the AI code generation tool:\n- Features to skip or stub out for now (e.g., ''skip email notifications, just log to console'')\n- Performance considerations (''use server components where possible'')\n- Specific patterns to follow or avoid\n- Seed data or placeholder content to include\n- Any known limitations of the target platform to work around\n- Mobile responsiveness requirements"}]}' WHERE name = 'Spec for AI Prototyping' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Write for the engineers who will implement this. Include specific technology choices with clear rationale. Data structures should specify types, relationships, and constraints. API sections should include request/response examples. Address scalability for realistic growth scenarios. Deployment steps should be concrete and reproducible.", "sections": [{"name": "Product Overview", "guidelines": "Context for the technical design.\n\n### Purpose\n- What problem this solves and the opportunity it captures\n- Specific user scenarios that illustrate the need\n\n### Target Audience\n- Key user personas and their needs\n- How the product addresses their pain points\n\n### Expected Outcomes\n- Tangible and intangible benefits\n- Key metrics / KPIs that will measure success\n- Short-term vs. long-term impact"}, {"name": "Architecture", "guidelines": "The structural design of the system.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\nDescribe how the major components connect. Include:\n- Frontend, backend, database, message queue, external services\n- Communication protocols (REST, GraphQL, WebSocket, gRPC)\n- Design patterns used (MVC, event-driven, CQRS, etc.)\n\n### Data Structures & Algorithms\n- Core data structures with justification for selection\n- Key algorithms and their complexity characteristics\n- Scalability and performance considerations\n\n### System Interfaces\n- **API endpoints**: Key routes with methods and purpose\n- **Third-party integrations**: External services and protocols\n- **Internal modules**: How components communicate internally\n\n### User Interface\n- Main UI components and interaction patterns\n- Wireframes or mockups (reference or describe)\n- Accessibility and responsive design approach"}, {"name": "Data Model", "guidelines": "How data is structured, stored, and accessed.\n\n### Entities\nFor each core entity:\n```\nEntityName\n- field: type (constraints)\n- field: type (constraints)\n```\n\n### Relationships\n- Entity A → Entity B: relationship type and FK behavior\n- Join tables for many-to-many relationships\n\n### Storage\n- Database technology and hosting\n- Caching strategy (Redis, in-memory, CDN)\n- File/blob storage approach\n\n### Data Flow\nHow data moves through the system from user input to persistence and back."}, {"name": "Testing Plan", "guidelines": "How the product will be validated at every level.\n\n### Testing Strategy\n- **Unit tests**: What is tested, framework, coverage targets\n- **Integration tests**: API and service interaction testing\n- **E2E tests**: Critical user flow testing, framework (Playwright, Cypress)\n- **Performance tests**: Load testing approach and targets\n\n### Testing Tools\n- Frameworks: [e.g., Vitest, Jest, Playwright]\n- CI integration: How tests run automatically\n- Code coverage: Targets and enforcement\n\n### Key Test Cases\nCritical scenarios to validate:\n- Happy path for core user flows\n- Edge cases and error handling\n- Security-sensitive operations\n- Performance under expected load\n\n### Reporting\n- How test results are tracked and communicated\n- Dashboard or tool for ongoing visibility"}, {"name": "Deployment Plan", "guidelines": "How the product moves from code to production.\n\n### Environment Setup\n- **Development**: Local setup, seed data, hot reload\n- **Staging**: Configuration, data sources, access control\n- **Production**: Infrastructure, scaling, monitoring\n\n### CI/CD Pipeline\n- Build steps: lint, type check, test, build\n- Deployment trigger: merge to main, manual, scheduled\n- Preview deployments for PRs\n\n### Deploy Process\n1. [Step with verification checkpoint]\n2. [Step with verification checkpoint]\n3. [Step with verification checkpoint]\n\n### Rollback Strategy\n- How to revert a bad deploy\n- Database migration rollback approach\n- Feature flags for gradual rollout\n\n### Post-Deploy Verification\n- Smoke tests to run after deploy\n- Monitoring dashboards to watch\n- Alert thresholds for automatic rollback"}, {"name": "Security & Performance", "guidelines": "Non-functional requirements and how they are addressed.\n\n### Security\n- Authentication and authorization approach\n- Input validation and sanitization\n- Secrets management (env vars, vault)\n- Dependency vulnerability scanning\n- OWASP Top 10 considerations relevant to this product\n\n### Performance\n- **Targets**: Page load < [X]s, API response < [X]ms, throughput > [X] req/s\n- **Optimization**: Caching, lazy loading, code splitting, CDN\n- **Monitoring**: APM tools, error tracking, custom metrics\n- **Scaling**: Horizontal vs. vertical, auto-scaling configuration\n\n### Observability\n- Logging strategy: What is logged, structured format, retention\n- Metrics: Key dashboards and what they track\n- Alerting: What triggers alerts and who responds"}]}' WHERE name = 'Technical Design Document' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Design tests that will change product decisions — avoid testing just to check a box. Participant criteria should reflect real user segments, not convenience samples. Tasks should be realistic end-to-end scenarios. Define specific pass/fail thresholds. Include a decision framework: what concrete changes will be made based on which outcomes.", "sections": [{"name": "Test Overview", "guidelines": "Why you are running this test and what you hope to learn.\n\n### Purpose\n- What usability questions this test will answer\n- What product decisions depend on the results\n\n### Scope\n- Which product area, feature, or flow is being tested\n- What is NOT being tested (out of scope)\n\n### Stakeholders\n| Role | Person | Responsibility |\n|------|--------|----------------|\n| Test facilitator | [Name] | Runs sessions, asks questions |\n| Note-taker | [Name] | Captures observations in real time |\n| Observer(s) | [Names] | Watches sessions, adds notes |\n| Decision maker | [Name] | Acts on findings |"}, {"name": "Test Design", "guidelines": "How the test will be structured and conducted.\n\n### Methodology\n- **Type**: Moderated / Unmoderated / Remote / In-person\n- **Format**: Think-aloud, task-based, comparative (A vs. B), exploratory\n- **Duration**: [X] minutes per session\n- **Number of sessions**: [X] participants\n\n### Participant Criteria\n- **Include**: [Demographics, experience level, user segment]\n- **Exclude**: [Who should NOT participate and why]\n- **Recruitment source**: Customer list, panel service, social media\n- **Incentive**: [What participants receive]\n\n### Test Environment\n- **Device/browser**: Specific requirements\n- **Prototype or live product**: Which version is being tested\n- **Tools**: UserTesting, Maze, Lookback, Zoom, etc."}, {"name": "Tasks & Scenarios", "guidelines": "What participants will be asked to do.\n\n### Scenarios\nRealistic contexts that set up the tasks (don''t hint at the UI):\n\n**Scenario 1: [Name]**\n''Imagine you are [context]. You need to [goal].''\n\n### Tasks\nFor each task:\n- **Task**: [What the participant should accomplish]\n- **Scenario**: [Which scenario it belongs to]\n- **Starting point**: [Where they begin]\n- **Success criteria**: [How you know they completed it]\n- **Time limit**: [Max time before moving on]\n\n### Post-Task Questions\n- On a scale of 1-7, how easy was that task?\n- What was confusing or unclear?\n- What would you expect to happen next?"}, {"name": "Metrics & Success Criteria", "guidelines": "How results will be measured.\n\n### Quantitative Metrics\n| Metric | Target | How Measured |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Task completion rate | > [X]% | [Method] |\n| Time on task | < [X] min | [Method] |\n| Error rate | < [X]% | [Method] |\n| Satisfaction (post-task) | > [X]/7 | Rating scale |\n| SUS score | > [X] | Post-test survey |\n\n### Qualitative Metrics\n- Confusion points: Where users hesitate, backtrack, or express frustration\n- Mental model fit: Does the UI match what users expect?\n- Feature discovery: Do users notice key features without prompting?\n- Emotional response: Confidence, frustration, delight at key moments\n\n### Pass/Fail Thresholds\n- **Pass**: [Criteria] → Proceed to launch\n- **Conditional pass**: [Criteria] → Fix issues, retest specific areas\n- **Fail**: [Criteria] → Redesign required before launch"}, {"name": "Session Guide", "guidelines": "Script and structure for running each test session.\n\n### Intro (5 min)\n- Welcome and set expectations\n- Explain think-aloud protocol\n- Confirm recording consent\n- Remind them: ''We are testing the product, not you''\n\n### Warm-Up (5 min)\n- Background questions about their role and experience\n- Current workflow related to the product area\n\n### Tasks (30-40 min)\n- Walk through each task/scenario in order\n- Ask post-task rating after each task\n- Note where they struggle without intervening\n\n### Debrief (10 min)\n- Overall impressions\n- What was easiest? Most frustrating?\n- Would they use this? Why/why not?\n- Any features they expected but didn''t find?"}, {"name": "Analysis & Recommendations", "guidelines": "How findings become product decisions.\n\n### Analysis Framework\nFor each finding:\n- **Finding**: [What was observed]\n- **Severity**: Critical / Major / Minor / Cosmetic\n- **Frequency**: [X] of [Y] participants encountered this\n- **Impact**: Effect on task completion and satisfaction\n- **Recommendation**: Specific change to make\n\n### Deliverables\n- **Findings summary**: All issues ranked by severity × frequency\n- **Highlight reel**: 3-5 min video of key moments for stakeholders\n- **Recommendation deck**: Prioritized changes with effort estimates\n\n### Decision Framework\n- **Critical**: Must fix before launch — blocks core functionality\n- **Major**: Fix in next sprint — degrades experience significantly\n- **Minor**: Add to backlog — nice-to-have improvements\n- **Cosmetic**: Fix when convenient — polish items\n\n### Next Steps\n- Schedule follow-up test to validate fixes (if needed)\n- Timeline for implementing changes\n- When to test again"}]}' WHERE name = 'Usability Test Plan' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Create vivid, research-grounded personas that feel like real people — not marketing stereotypes. Include specific behavioral details that directly inform product decisions. Quotes should sound natural and reveal genuine frustrations or aspirations. Each persona should have distinct needs that lead to different product implications.", "sections": [{"name": "Persona Name", "guidelines": "A memorable, descriptive name that the team can reference in discussions.\n\n### Format\n- **Name**: A real-sounding first name + a descriptive qualifier\n- **Example**: ''Startup Sarah'' / ''Enterprise Eli'' / ''Freelancer Fran''\n- **Photo suggestion**: Describe the type of stock photo that represents this persona\n\nThe name should instantly evoke who this person is — make it stick in the team''s memory."}, {"name": "Demographics", "guidelines": "Factual information that sets the context for this persona''s needs and behaviors.\n\n### Key Details\n- **Age range**: e.g., 28-35\n- **Job title & seniority**: e.g., Senior Product Manager\n- **Company size**: Startup (<50) / Mid-market (50-500) / Enterprise (500+)\n- **Industry**: e.g., SaaS, E-commerce, Healthcare\n- **Location**: Urban/suburban/remote, region\n- **Technical skill**: Non-technical / Technical-adjacent / Developer\n\nOnly include demographics that actually influence product decisions — skip what doesn''t matter."}, {"name": "Goals", "guidelines": "What this persona is trying to achieve — both with your product and in their broader role.\n\n### Product Goals\nWhat they want to accomplish using your product:\n- Goal 1: [Specific outcome they want]\n- Goal 2: [Another specific outcome]\n\n### Career/Role Goals\nBroader objectives that your product helps with:\n- [Underlying motivation: look competent, save time, get promoted, reduce stress]\n\n### Jobs to Be Done\nThe ''job'' they are ''hiring'' your product to do:\n- **When I** [situation], **I want to** [motivation], **so I can** [expected outcome]"}, {"name": "Pain Points", "guidelines": "Specific frustrations this persona experiences — grounded in research, not assumptions.\n\n### Current Frustrations\nFor each pain point:\n- **Pain**: What specifically frustrates them\n- **Frequency**: How often they encounter this\n- **Severity**: Annoying / Painful / Blocking\n- **Current workaround**: What they do today to cope\n\n### Quotes from Research\nDirect quotes that capture the frustration in their own words:\n- *\"I spend half my Monday just pulling together the weekly numbers from three different tools.\"*\n- *\"I know there''s a better way but I don''t have time to figure it out.\"*"}, {"name": "User Journey", "guidelines": "How this persona typically interacts with your product category.\n\n### Discovery\n- How they first learn about products like yours\n- Where they go for information (Google, communities, peers, Twitter)\n\n### Evaluation\n- What criteria matter most when comparing options\n- Who else is involved in the decision (manager, team, IT)\n\n### Adoption\n- First actions they take when trying a new tool\n- What determines whether they stick with it or churn\n\n### Ongoing Usage\n- Typical frequency and depth of use\n- What triggers them to engage (notification, workflow, habit)"}, {"name": "Behavioral Traits", "guidelines": "Key characteristics that influence how this persona uses products.\n\n### Technology Behavior\n- **Tool adoption**: Early adopter / Pragmatist / Late majority\n- **Learning style**: Explores independently / Reads docs / Needs hand-holding\n- **Platform preference**: Mobile-first / Desktop / Both equally\n\n### Work Style\n- **Decision making**: Data-driven / Intuition-based / Consensus-seeking\n- **Communication**: Slack/async / Meetings / Email\n- **Pace**: Moves fast and iterates / Plans carefully before acting\n\n### Personality\n2-3 key adjectives that define how they approach problems (e.g., detail-oriented, pragmatic, skeptical, collaborative)."}, {"name": "Quotes from User Research", "guidelines": "Direct quotes from interviews, surveys, or support conversations that bring this persona to life.\n\n### Format\n*\"[Quote that reveals a key insight about their needs or frustrations]\"*\n— Context: [Where/when this was said]\n\n### Guidelines\n- Include 3-5 quotes that each reveal something different\n- Mix positive quotes (what they love) with negative (what frustrates them)\n- Quotes should feel real — messy, specific, and human\n- If using hypothetical quotes, base them closely on actual research themes"}, {"name": "Product Implications", "guidelines": "What this persona means for product decisions — the actionable takeaway.\n\n### Design Implications\n- UI complexity level this persona can handle\n- Key workflows to optimize for this persona\n- Onboarding approach that matches their learning style\n\n### Feature Priority\n- Features this persona values most\n- Features this persona doesn''t care about\n- Gaps in the current product for this persona\n\n### Messaging\n- Value proposition that resonates with this persona\n- Language and tone that works (technical, simple, aspirational)\n- What competitors this persona is comparing you to"}]}' WHERE name = 'User Personas' AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates SET structure = '{"instructions": "Design a practical testing plan that will actually inform product decisions. Test scenarios should reflect real user workflows, not contrived tasks. Include specific questions for post-task interviews. Metrics should be directly tied to ship/no-ship decisions. Describe what changes would be made based on different outcome ranges.", "sections": [{"name": "Test Objectives", "guidelines": "What you want to learn from this test — not what you want to prove.\n\n### Primary Questions\n1. Can users successfully [complete key task] without assistance?\n2. Where do users get confused or stuck?\n3. Does the [feature/flow] match users'' mental model?\n\n### Success Criteria\nDefine specific thresholds that determine pass/fail:\n- **Task completion rate**: > [X]%\n- **Time on task**: < [X] minutes\n- **Error rate**: < [X]%\n- **Satisfaction score**: > [X]/7"}, {"name": "Participant Criteria", "guidelines": "Who to recruit for the test.\n\n### Target Profile\n- **Number of participants**: 5-8 per user segment\n- **Key demographics**: Role, experience level, industry\n- **Required experience**: Familiarity with [tool/workflow/domain]\n- **Exclusions**: People who [shouldn''t be included and why]\n\n### Recruitment\n- **Source**: Customer list, user panel, recruitment service, social media\n- **Screening questions**: 3-5 questions to qualify participants\n- **Incentive**: What participants receive for their time"}, {"name": "Test Scenarios", "guidelines": "Realistic scenarios that set context for the tasks — not step-by-step instructions.\n\n### Format\n**Scenario [#]: [Name]**\nImagine you are [role/context]. You need to [goal]. [Additional context that makes it realistic].\n\n### Guidelines\n- Scenarios should reflect actual use cases from user research\n- Don''t hint at the UI — describe the goal, not the steps\n- Include one ''easy'' scenario and one ''challenging'' scenario\n- Cover both first-time and repeat usage if relevant"}, {"name": "Tasks", "guidelines": "Specific tasks participants will complete within each scenario.\n\n### Format\n**Task [#]**: [What the participant should accomplish]\n- **Scenario**: Which scenario this belongs to\n- **Starting point**: Where the user begins (specific URL/screen)\n- **Success criteria**: How you know they completed it\n- **Time limit**: Maximum time before moving on\n\n### Task Types\n- **Discovery tasks**: Can you find [information]?\n- **Completion tasks**: Complete [workflow] end to end\n- **Comparison tasks**: Which of these options would you choose?\n- **Open-ended tasks**: How would you approach [goal]?"}, {"name": "Metrics for Success", "guidelines": "Quantitative and qualitative measures to evaluate the test results.\n\n### Quantitative Metrics\n- **Task completion rate**: % who complete each task successfully\n- **Time on task**: Average and range for each task\n- **Error rate**: Number and types of errors per task\n- **Clicks/steps**: Number of actions to complete each task\n- **SUS/satisfaction score**: Post-test questionnaire results\n\n### Qualitative Metrics\n- **Think-aloud observations**: Confusion points, expressed frustrations\n- **Feature discovery**: Did users notice [feature] without prompting?\n- **Mental model fit**: Did the interface match user expectations?\n- **Emotional response**: Confidence, frustration, delight at key moments"}, {"name": "Feedback Collection", "guidelines": "How data will be gathered during and after the test.\n\n### During the Test\n- **Think-aloud protocol**: Participant narrates their thought process\n- **Screen recording**: Capture all interactions and mouse movements\n- **Observer notes**: Facilitator and observers capture key moments\n- **Task timing**: Start/stop times for each task\n\n### Post-Test\n- **Questionnaire**: SUS, NPS, or custom satisfaction survey\n- **Debrief interview**: 5-10 open-ended questions about the experience\n- **Preference ranking**: If testing multiple designs, rank preference\n\n### Tools\nSpecify the usability testing tools: UserTesting, Maze, Lookback, Zoom, etc."}, {"name": "Analysis & Recommendations", "guidelines": "How findings will be analyzed and turned into product decisions.\n\n### Analysis Framework\n- **Severity rating** for each finding: Critical / Major / Minor / Cosmetic\n- **Frequency**: How many participants encountered each issue\n- **Impact**: Effect on task completion and user satisfaction\n\n### Deliverables\n- **Findings report**: Summary of all issues with severity and frequency\n- **Highlight reel**: 3-5 minute video of key moments for stakeholders\n- **Recommendations**: Prioritized list of changes to make\n\n### Decision Framework\n- **Critical findings**: Must fix before launch\n- **Major findings**: Fix in next sprint\n- **Minor findings**: Add to backlog\n- **No issues found**: Validate with larger sample or proceed"}]}' WHERE name = 'User Testing Plan' AND is_built_in = true;
